    "text": "4.4 Equity — residual after liabilities Equity is the residual interest in the assets of the entity after deducting all its liabilities. Equity claims are claims on that residual — claims against the entity that do not meet the definition of a liability. The Framework does not start from instrument labels; the liability definition comes first and what remains is equity. CF 2018 · para 4.63–4.64 CF 2018 · para BC4.89 PwC MOA 2020 · 1.72 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · section 7.4 Decision — liability or equity claim? Test Does the claim against the entity meet the definition of a liability — a present obligation to transfer an economic resource as a result of past events? CF 2018 · para 4.26–4.27 CF 2018 · para 4.64 Yes Outcome · Yes Liability. CF 2018 · para 4.26 No Outcome · No Equity claim — established by contract, legislation or similar means, including shares of various types and some obligations to issue another equity claim, to the extent they do not meet the liability definition. CF 2018 · para 4.64 Pitfall: treating every share as equity — shares and obligations to issue equity claims are equity claims only to the extent that they do not meet the definition of a liability. CF 2018 · para 4.64 Classes of equity claim confer different rights, components of equity can reflect legal restrictions, and the definition applies to all reporting entities. The binary distinction was kept because at least two classes of claim are unavoidable, and presentation of equity classes is left to Chapter 7. Classes of equity claim confer different rights. Ordinary and preference shares may give rights to (a) Dividends, if the entity decides to pay them to eligible holders; (b) The proceeds of satisfying the claims, in full on liquidation or in part at other times; or (c) Other equity claims. CF 2018 · para 4.65 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · section 7.4 Components of equity and legal restrictions. Legal, regulatory or other requirements may affect components such as share capital or retained earnings — for example, permitting distributions only from reserves specified as distributable. Equity can be sub-classified into types of capital and reserves reflecting legal restrictions or the differing rights of owners. CF 2018 · para 4.66 PwC MOA 2020 · 1.72 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · section 7.4 All reporting entities. Sole proprietorships, partnerships, trusts and government business undertakings often have different legal frameworks — perhaps few restrictions on distributions — but the definition of equity applies to all reporting entities. CF 2018 · para 4.67 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · section 7.4 Binary distinction kept — at least two classes of claim are unavoidable. A binary split makes a single distinction between claims with various characteristics, but unless all claims are measured directly at least one residual class is needed, and measuring all claims directly would require valuing the entire entity. A third element was rejected as making classification more complex and needing a decision on whether its changes are income or expenses; separate presentation of classes within liabilities or equity can achieve a similar outcome. CF 2018 · para BC4.89–BC4.91 Presentation of equity classes is left to Chapter 7. The Framework does not discuss presentation and disclosure for different classes of equity claims and components of equity in Chapter 4. CF 2018 · para BC4.89(c) CF 2018 · para 7.12–7.13 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · section 10.2.2 Out of scope — distinguishing liabilities from equity instruments. The 2018 Framework does not address classification of financial instruments with characteristics of both liabilities and equity; the Board explores this in its Financial Instruments with Characteristics of Equity research project, which may lead to changes to the definitions. Controversial areas such as the liability–equity distinction were removed from the revised Framework and are dealt with in separate projects. The obligation guidance in paragraphs 4.28–4.35 was not designed to distinguish liabilities from equity and is unlikely to change. CF 2018 · para BC4.45 CF 2018 · para BC4.92 DTT A2 2022 · 7 KPMG New Foundation 2018 · p1"
