    "chapter": "Chapter 4 — Elements",
    "href": "../cf-2018/draft-v1-linked/ch04.html#s-4-5",
    "text": "4.5 Income and expenses — changes in assets and liabilities, excluding holders of equity claims Income and expenses are defined through changes in assets and liabilities that change equity, other than contributions from or distributions to holders of equity claims. Income is increases in assets, or decreases in liabilities, that result in increases in equity, other than those relating to contributions from holders of equity claims. Expenses are decreases in assets, or increases in liabilities, that result in decreases in equity, other than those relating to distributions to holders of equity claims. It follows that contributions from holders of equity claims are not income, and distributions to them are not expenses. CF 2018 · para 4.68–4.70 PwC MOA 2020 · 1.78–1.79 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · section 7.5 Sources differ — label for the income and expense wording Source Treatment Official Paragraphs 4.68–4.69 are the definitions of income and expenses; recognition criteria are set separately in Chapter 5. CF 2018 · para 4.68–4.69 CF 2018 · para 5.6–5.7 PwC Introduces the same quoted wording as “the recognition criterion for income” and “the recognition criterion for expenses”. PwC MOA 2020 · 1.78–1.79 Shown side by side and not reconciled. Assessment — which element does a change in assets or liabilities give? Test Does the change in assets or liabilities change equity — and if so, does it relate to holders of equity claims? CF 2018 · para 4.2 CF 2018 · para 4.68–4.70 Changes Outcome · not with equity-claim holders Income (equity increases) or expenses (equity decreases). The sale of goods for cash gives both: income from recognising the cash and an expense from derecognising the goods sold. CF 2018 · para 4.68–4.69 CF 2018 · para 5.5 Owners Outcome · with equity-claim holders Neither income nor expenses — contributions from holders of equity claims and distributions to them. CF 2018 · para 4.70 No change Outcome · equity unchanged Neither income nor expenses — an exchange of assets or liabilities that does not increase or decrease equity. CF 2018 · para 4.2 Matching is not an objective, yet performance information is just as important. Performance is defined through assets and liabilities, income and expenses with different characteristics are presented separately, and no subclasses are defined. Matching is not an objective — but performance information is just as important. The asset–liability approach replaced matching as the driver of recognition; users need information about both financial position and financial performance, so information about income and expenses is just as important as information about assets and liabilities. Matching arises only when it follows from recognising changes in assets and liabilities. CF 2018 · para 4.71 CF 2018 · para 5.5 PwC MOA 2020 · 1.80 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · section 7.5 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · section 8.1 Why define performance through assets and liabilities. A few respondents argued this gives undue primacy to the statement of financial position. The Board disagreed: (a) It has not designated position or performance as the primary focus — it considers both in recognition, measurement, presentation and disclosure decisions; CF 2018 · para BC4.94(a) (b) Information about transactions is relevant, and much financial reporting is and will remain transaction-based; CF 2018 · para BC4.94(b) (c) Transactions that give income and expenses also change assets and liabilities, so defining assets and liabilities first is more effective, efficient and rigorous; CF 2018 · para BC4.94(c) (d) Assets and liabilities refer to real economic phenomena, whereas amounts arising as by-products of matching do not necessarily depict economic phenomena; CF 2018 · para BC4.94(d) (e) Matching does not define the period to which income and expenses relate, and an intention to match does not justify recognising items that do not meet the asset or liability definitions. CF 2018 · para BC4.94(e) CF 2018 · para BC4.93–BC4.94 Different characteristics — separate presentation. Different transactions and events generate income and expenses with different characteristics; providing information about them separately helps users understand performance. CF 2018 · para 4.72 CF 2018 · para 7.14–7.19 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · section 7.5 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · section 10.2.3 No subclasses defined. The 2010 discussion of revenue, gains and losses was not carried forward — the emphasis that income includes revenue and gains and expenses include losses is unnecessary, and implying that the Framework defines subclasses is unhelpful; no change in practice is expected. CF 2018 · para BC4.96 Illustration: 4.5.1 — Call charges invoiced after the month — income when the effects occur PwC MOA 2020 · FAQ 1.78.1 CF 2018 · para 4.68 CF 2018 · para 5.5 CF 2018 · para 1.17 Facts Entity G, a telecom entity, invoices customers monthly for call charges. At the beginning of March it sends invoices totalling C500,000 for calls customers made in February . The invoices are payable by the end of April. Conclusion Recognise income of C500,000 at the end of February in the income statement. Recognise a corresponding increase in assets (accounts receivable) in the balance sheet. Basis Accrual accounting: the effects of transactions and other events are recognised when they occur and reported in the periods to which they relate. Not timed to cash or invoicing: revenue is not accelerated or deferred to match the receipt of cash or the raising of invoices."
