    "href": "../cf-2018/draft-v1-linked/ch09.html#s-9-7",
    "text": "9.7 Historical or pro forma, presentation and disclosure Combined statements that claim IFRS compliance rest on historical data. Historical financial information is past information prepared under statutory requirements and accounting standards, drawn from distinct or separate accounting records, and auditable. Pro forma information is hypothetical: it illustrates an outcome on an “as-if” basis, including effects of events that did not occur or occurred at a different date. PwC MOA 2020 · A2.63–A2.64 Decision — historical information or pro forma? Test Do the amounts and adjustments rest on actual past amounts — including actual group expenses allocated on a rational basis — rather than “as-if” effects of events that did not occur? PwC MOA 2020 · A2.64 PwC MOA 2020 · A2.67–A2.68 Historic Outcome · Historical IFRS compliance can be claimed if the other requirements are met. Adjustments and allocations for items the new reporting entity needs are frequent; deciding what is historical is a key judgement. PwC MOA 2020 · A2.63 PwC MOA 2020 · A2.66–A2.68 As-if Outcome · As-if Pro forma — does not comply with IFRS. Some jurisdictions regard any allocation as a pro forma adjustment. PwC MOA 2020 · A2.64 PwC MOA 2020 · A2.67 PwC MOA 2020 · A2.69 A neat sub-group is rare, and comparative periods are presented without hindsight. A neat sub-group is rare. A new reporting entity is rarely a sub-group whose consolidated statements already present all and only its activities — including debt, taxes, share-based payments, share of corporate assets, management services and charges; where it is, it can present consolidated statements. PwC MOA 2020 · A2.65 No hindsight. Comparative periods stay closed and the business is presented as management ran it at the time — accruals for a lawsuit settled before the combined statements are prepared are not trued up. PwC MOA 2020 · A2.132 Illustration: 9.7.1 — Disposal of a sub-group with parent PPA goodwill — historical information PwC MOA 2020 · Exhibit A2.7 PwC MOA 2020 · A2.63 Facts Group X intends to dispose of sub-group Y together with subsidiary Z, another directly owned subsidiary. X acquired Y four years ago; its purchase price allocation recognised goodwill in X’s IFRS consolidated statements. Management prepares combined statements for Y and Z using the amounts in X’s consolidated statements. Assessment The goodwill and other PPA adjustments in X’s consolidated statements are included in the sub-group financial statements. The combined statements are based on financial statements prepared using only historical information. Conclusion It might be possible to claim compliance with IFRS. Illustration: 9.7.2 — Metals demerger presented as a stand-alone listed group — pro forma PwC MOA 2020 · Exhibit A2.8 PwC MOA 2020 · A2.64 PwC MOA 2020 · A2.69 Facts Group X has metals and coal operations and plans a demerger creating two listed entities, A Metals and A Coal; each shareholder’s investment is divided between them. A Metals combined statements are prepared from historical information extracted from X’s audited statements and underlying records. Income statements and cash flows are presented as if the demerger had occurred and A Metals had been a stand-alone group for the three-year track record the listing authority requires. Assessment Coal activities in the same legal entities as metals in some jurisdictions are excluded. Impairments recognised during the track record are reversed against opening retained earnings. Additional costs are recorded to present A Metals as a separate listed entity, without regard to X’s actual expenses. Conclusion The information is prepared on a pro forma basis and is not in accordance with IFRS. Goodwill and purchase-price adjustments should accompany the business they relate to. The predecessor method for common-control combinations generally uses carrying values at the highest level of common control, and the current parent is unlikely to be part of the new reporting entity. Parents rarely allocate goodwill in separate IFRS statements — IFRS has no push-down requirement, and company law often makes push-down difficult. Many users and regulators would see the track record as incomplete without the cost of the business to the current parent, so goodwill and related adjustments might be included rather than stranded in the parent. A carve-out goodwill note presents allocated goodwill by segment as if the group had acquired the business at the acquisition date. PwC MOA 2020 · A2.74–A2.78 PwC MOA 2020 · Exhibit A2.9 Whether to present NCI turns on factors such as whether it survives the planned transaction, and the treatment chosen must be useful and comply with IFRS. Non-controlling interest — practice varies. Whether to present it depends on factors including whether the NCI survives the planned transaction; choose a treatment that is useful and complies with IFRS. PwC MOA 2020 · A2.52 Businesses that will leave the new legal group are presented on a carve-out basis or a disposal group basis. The track-record entity may include businesses that will not continue after a re-organisation near the transaction. Regulators may prefer one approach — US regulators would generally expect the disposal group basis — and users often prefer information on the continuing business only. PwC MOA 2020 · A2.53–A2.56 Decision — carve-out basis or disposal group basis? Test Is the business being disposed of dissimilar to the rest of the new reporting entity, separately managed, and supported by separate historical books and records? PwC MOA 2020 · A2.56 All met Outcome · All criteria met Carve-out basis is reasonable — exclude all businesses and activity that will not form part of the new legal group. The criteria are not defined in IFRS; UK and US regulators have more explicit guidance sharing similar concepts. PwC MOA 2020 · A2.54 PwC MOA 2020 · A2.56 Not met Outcome · Not met Disposal group basis — include those businesses up to disposal or discontinuance, presented separately from the continuing business. The disposal group is similar to IFRS 5’s, need not be a discontinued operation, and applies only to balance sheets where IFRS 5’s requirements were met at that date. PwC MOA 2020 · A2.57–A2.61 Clear disclosure is fundamental. The notes should address at least: PwC MOA 2020 · A2.139–A2.140 Purpose for which the combined statements are prepared PwC MOA 2020 · A2.140 How the new reporting entity was identified and the significant judgements — the boundary described in the basis of preparation, explaining which entities are included — and a list of combining entities PwC MOA 2020 · A2.140 CF 2018 · para 3.14 Whether prepared in accordance with IFRS or a basis of preparation PwC MOA 2020 · A2.140 Principal accounting policies How assets, liabilities, income and expenses were allocated How inter-company transactions were reflected PwC MOA 2020 · A2.140 How EPS was calculated, if applicable Critical judgements and estimates PwC MOA 2020 · A2.140 Disclosure requirements of all applicable IFRSs, if IFRS compliance is claimed PwC MOA 2020 · A2.140"
