    "chapter": "Chapter 10 — Carve-out financial statements",
    "href": "../cf-2018/draft-v1-linked/ch10.html#s-10-1",
    "text": "10.1 Reporting entity — a portion of an entity A reporting entity can be a portion of an entity; when it is not a legal entity, primary users’ information needs set its boundary. CF 2018 · para 3.10 CF 2018 · para 3.13–3.14 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · section 6.2 A portion of an entity is allowed, but its boundary can be difficult and must pass faithful representation tests. Portions are why the Board tightened the boundary, and legal entities normally set it. Portion allowed. A reporting entity can be a single entity, a portion of an entity , or more than one entity — not necessarily a legal entity. CF 2018 · para 3.10 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · section 6.2 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 1.2.90.10 Difficult boundary. If the reporting entity is not a legal entity and does not comprise only legal entities linked by a parent–subsidiary relationship, its boundary is driven by the information needs of its primary users. CF 2018 · para 3.13–3.14 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · section 6.2 Faithful representation tests. The boundary must not contain an arbitrary or incomplete set of economic activities; including that set must give neutral information; and the statements must describe how the boundary was determined and what constitutes the reporting entity. See also 9.2 Difficult boundaries CF 2018 · para 3.14 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · section 6.2 KPMG Combined/Carve 2022 · 1.2 Portions are why the Board tightened the boundary. Respondents to the 2015 Exposure Draft worried that a portion of an entity could report an incomplete set of economic activities — for example, by excluding its share of overheads — and that identifying which claims to include could be difficult. The Board made completeness and neutrality particularly important in setting the boundary: an arbitrary or incomplete set gives information that is incomplete, may lack neutrality and does not meet users’ needs. CF 2018 · para BC3.17–BC3.18 Legal entities normally set the boundary. Determining the boundary is normally straightforward when the reporting entity is a legal entity, or comprises only legal entities all linked by parent–subsidiary relationships — the legal boundary then meets users’ needs. A portion of an entity falls outside that case. CF 2018 · para BC3.19 Decision — is the reporting entity a portion of an entity? Test Is the reporting entity a portion of an entity — not a legal entity, and not only legal entities linked by a parent–subsidiary relationship? CF 2018 · para 3.10 CF 2018 · para 3.13 Yes Outcome · Portion Carve-out financial statements. Primary users’ information needs set the boundary, which must pass the faithful representation tests (10.3). CF 2018 · para 3.13–3.14 PwC MOA 2020 · A2.5 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · section 6.2 No Outcome · Not a portion Single-entity, consolidated, unconsolidated or combined financial statements. A legal entity, or legal entities all linked by parent–subsidiary relationships, normally sets the boundary; two or more entities not all so linked → combined. See also 3.6 9.1 CF 2018 · para 3.10–3.12 CF 2018 · para BC3.19 Although the Framework does not say when carve-out statements could be prepared, considerations for combined statements carry over to carve-outs, and combined-statement guidance addresses the carve-out entity directly. The Framework does not say when carve-out statements could be prepared. The Board gives a general description of a reporting entity rather than stating who must, should or could prepare general purpose financial statements — it has no authority to determine that — so preparation rests on judgement, firm practice and regulation. CF 2018 · para BC3.13 CF 2018 · para BC3.21 DTT A2 2022 · 6.2 Considerations for combined statements carry over to carve-outs. The Framework is silent on carve-out accounting, where a reporting entity is determined to be part of a legal entity; in EY’s view, considerations similar to those the Framework discusses for combined financial statements should apply to statements prepared on a carve-out basis. See also 9.1 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · section 6.2 Combined-statement guidance addresses the carve-out entity directly. PwC’s guidance on combined statements applies to the carve-out entity for interest, impairments and cash flows, and treats clear disclosure as fundamental to both combined and carve-out statements. PwC MOA 2020 · A2.104 PwC MOA 2020 · A2.113 PwC MOA 2020 · A2.133 PwC MOA 2020 · A2.139"
