    "chapter": "Chapter 10 — Carve-out financial statements",
    "href": "../cf-2018/draft-v1-linked/ch10.html#s-10-2",
    "text": "10.2 Firm definitions — carve-out Firms describe a carve-out as a division or lesser business component of a larger entity and as smaller components carved from a larger reporting entity, contrast it with combined financial statements, and note that it is rarely a neat sub-group. A division or lesser business component of a larger entity. The term arises because the unit’s statements are viewed as “carved out” of the larger consolidated financial statements. There is no accounting definition of a carve-out transaction; the term is commonly used for equity carve-outs, spin-offs and split-offs. PwC MOA 2020 · A2.5 PwC MOA 2020 · A2.17 Smaller components carved from a larger reporting entity. The term reflects that smaller components — e.g. unincorporated divisions — are carved out from a larger reporting entity. KPMG Combined/Carve 2022 · 1.3 Typical contrasts with combined financial statements. Generally not whole legal entities only; may lack full stand-alone historical ledgers; more pervasive allocations; may need parent support to operate. See also 9.5 KPMG Combined/Carve 2022 · 1.3 Rarely a neat sub-group. A new reporting entity is rarely a sub-group that has prepared consolidated statements presenting only and all of its activities — including debt, taxes, share-based payments, its share of corporate assets, and management services and charges. Most need adjustments, including allocating assets, liabilities and expenses that are relevant to the new reporting entity but not accounted for in the legal entities inside its boundary. PwC MOA 2020 · A2.65–A2.66 Illustration: 10.2.1 — Carve-out of a cable division from one legal entity KPMG Combined/Carve 2022 · Example 1C CF 2018 · para 3.10 CF 2018 · para 3.13–3.14 PwC MOA 2020 · A2.5 KPMG Combined/Carve 2022 · 1.3 KPMG Combined/Carve 2022 · 4.2 CF 2018 · para 3.14 PwC MOA 2020 · A2.7–A2.9 CF 2018 · para BC3.21 Facts Owner D has a cable and telephone business inside a single legal entity (Telecoms LLC). D prepares carve-out financial statements for the CableTV Division only, in preparation for an IPO. The Telephone Division remains outside the carve-out reporting entity. The cable division is an unincorporated component of the larger entity — not a separate historical legal subgroup. Structure Legal entity Component In carve-out RE? Telecoms LLC (Owner D) CableTV Division (unincorporated) Yes — IPO pack Telecoms LLC (Owner D) Telephone Division No — remains outside CF shape Portion of an entity (3.10) Difficult boundary → 3.13–3.14 tests; allocations often pervasive Assessment CF portion-of-entity. Reporting entity can be a portion of an entity. Difficult boundary. Not a stand-alone legal entity and not a parent–subsidiary group of legal entities for “cable only” → apply para 3.13–3.14 tests. Firm label. Classic carve-out: smaller component carved from a larger reporting entity. Records / allocations. Expect more judgement on shared costs, shared assets, financing — allocations often more pervasive than whole-entity combined packs. Conclusion Carve-out FS for CableTV Division are consistent with CF allowing a portion-of-entity reporting entity. Describe the boundary (what is in / out; how set) so information is not an arbitrary incomplete set. IFRS claim depends on purpose, records quality, allocations, and regulator views — not automatic from the CF label alone."
