    "chapter": "Chapter 10 — Carve-out financial statements",
    "href": "../cf-2018/draft-v1-linked/ch10.html#s-10-3",
    "text": "10.3 Boundary — fit for purpose The carve-out boundary must give primary users relevant information that faithfully represents the carved reporting entity. CF 2018 · para 1.2 CF 2018 · para 3.14 KPMG Combined/Carve 2022 · 1.2 The boundary must be fit for purpose, and the binding-element examples sit in Chapter 9. Fit for purpose. Identify the economic activities included so the financial statements meet primary users’ needs. KPMG Combined/Carve 2022 · 2 Binding-element examples sit in Chapter 9. Common control and common management examples (Examples 2A–2C, 2E) are Illustrations 9.5.3–9.5.6; the carve-specific perimeter conflict is Illustration 10.3.1 (Example 2D). See also Illus 9.5.3 KPMG Combined/Carve 2022 · 2.3 CF 2018 · para 3.14 Decision — does the carve-out boundary faithfully represent the economic activities? Test Is the set of economic activities complete and non-arbitrary — including the portion’s share of overheads — and does including it give neutral information? CF 2018 · para 3.14 CF 2018 · para BC3.17 Yes Outcome · Yes Relevant, faithful information for primary users. Describe how the boundary was determined and what constitutes the reporting entity. CF 2018 · para 3.14 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · section 6.2 No Outcome · No The information is incomplete and may lack neutrality, so it does not meet users’ information needs. CF 2018 · para BC3.18 Illustration: 10.3.1 — Management track record vs undertaking of the issuer (health clubs) KPMG Combined/Carve 2022 · Example 2D CF 2018 · para 3.13–3.14 CF 2018 · para 1.2 CF 2018 · para BC3.21 Facts Company C owns and centrally manages 50 health clubs in one legal entity (40 profitable / 10 historically loss-making). C plans an IPO spin-off listing only the 40 successful clubs via a newco; regulation requires carve-out FS. In C’s filing jurisdiction, the management track-record concept is prevalent for offering documents. Some other jurisdictions instead emphasise the undertaking of the issuer (post-transaction operations only). Two lenses Lens Include 10 loss-makers? User need emphasised Management track record Yes — omit would be incomplete / non-neutral stewardship How management ran the brand historically Undertaking of the issuer No — only the 40 clubs that go public Post-transaction operations of the issuer Framework test (both lenses) Outcome follows which primary-user need the pack serves Relevant + faithfully representative boundary Assessment — two regulatory lenses Track-record lens. Excluding the 10 loss-makers would omit part of management’s historical stewardship of the brand — incomplete / non-neutral for primary users assessing track record. Include all 50 historically; present components that will not continue going forward under firm presentation guidance. Undertaking-of-issuer lens. Some regulators require excluding operations that will not go public — carve-out includes only the 40 clubs (consistency with post-IPO RE / future cash inflows). Both lenses rest on primary-user needs. Both argue primary-user information needs + faithful representation — outcome depends on which user need the pack is designed for; disclose the boundary. Conclusion Regulation and primary-user purpose drive the boundary between track record and undertaking. Research jurisdiction practice before locking the carve-out perimeter. See also Illus 9.5.6 — when combining is inappropriate"
