    "chapter": "Chapter 11 — Status & use with Standards",
    "href": "../cf-2018/draft-v1-linked/ch11.html#s-11-0",
    "text": "11.0 Scope of this chapter The Framework opens with a section on its status and purpose, ahead of its eight chapters and an appendix of defined terms. That section states what the Framework is for, that it is not a Standard and overrides none, how the Board departs from it or revises it, and the outcomes that Standards built on it serve. CF 2018 · para SP1.1–SP1.5 PwC MOA 2020 · 1.15 DTT A2 2022 · 3 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · section 3.1 The Framework has three purposes and does not override a Standard, it reflects the concepts that underlie financial statements for external users, and some Standards refer to it although it overrides none of them. Three purposes, and no override of a Standard. The purpose triad covers the Board, preparers and all parties; the Framework is not a Standard and cannot justify a treatment that contravenes one; IAS 8.11 sets the order preparers follow when no Standard applies. DTT A2 2022 · 2 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · section 3.2 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 1.2.10 The Framework reflects the concepts that underlie financial statements for external users. PwC sets out the same three purposes and states that the Framework is not a standard and does not override any standard. PwC MOA 2020 · 1.16–1.17 Some Standards refer to the Framework, but it overrides none of them. Nothing in the Framework overrides any specific standard or any requirement of one; however, some accounting standards make reference to it. EY iGAAP 2026 Ch2 · Overview See also 1.2 Objective 4.1 Elements 5.2 Recognition criteria 6.5 Selecting a measurement basis 7.5 Profit or loss and OCI"
