    "chapter": "Chapter 3 — Initial measurement",
    "href": "../ias-16/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-4",
    "text": "3.4 Dismantling, removal and restoration — initial estimate Cost includes the initial estimate of the costs of dismantling and removing the item and restoring the site on which it is located, the obligation for which an entity incurs either when the item is acquired or as a consequence of having used the item during a particular period for purposes other than to produce inventories during that period. Obligations arising from using the item to produce inventories are accounted for under IAS 2. Obligations are recognised and measured under IAS 37. IAS 16 · para 16(c) IAS 16 · para 18 PwC MOA 2020 Ch22 · 22.26 DTT iGAAP 2022 A7 · 4.2.3 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · section 4.1.11 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.2 · 3.2.70.10 Present obligation only. No provision (and no capitalisation) for environmental damage that has yet to occur from future operations — there is no present obligation for damage not yet caused. KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.2 · Example 4 IAS 37 · IE Example 3 Installation contamination vs inventory production. Clean-up from contamination caused by installation / testing is capitalised as part of the asset. Contamination from producing inventories is included in inventory cost (or expensed if abnormal) under IAS 2 / IAS 16 · para 18. KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.2 · Example 5 IAS 16 · para 18 Leasehold remediation (PwC). Dilapidation / make-good obligations under a lease (for example dismantling internal walls at lease end) create a provision under IAS 37; both the cost of building the improvement and the restoration estimate are capitalised when the improvement is built and depreciated over the useful life (generally the expected lease term). PwC MOA 2020 Ch22 · FAQ 22.26.1 IAS 16 · para 16(c) Changes after initial recognition — IFRIC 1 tip. Subsequent changes in estimated timing / amount of cash flows or discount rate for an existing decommissioning liability that was included in PPE cost are accounted for under IFRIC 1 (cost model vs revaluation model paths differ). Periodic unwinding of the discount is finance cost in profit or loss — not a borrowing cost under IAS 23. Detail of IFRIC 1 mechanics is see-also / Models-adjacent; this chapter owns the initial estimate. IFRIC 1 · para 2–5 PwC MOA 2020 Ch22 · 22.27–22.31 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · section 4.3 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.2 · 3.2.80 New obligations after acquisition. A law change that creates a removal obligation the entity did not face at acquisition is worked in Illustration 3.4.2 . DTT iGAAP 2022 A7 · 4.2.10-1 IAS 16 · para 7 IAS 16 · para BC14 See also — IAS 2 · IAS 37 · IFRIC 1. Inventory-production dismantling → IAS 2; measurement of the provision → IAS 37; subsequent changes in existing decommissioning liabilities → IFRIC 1 (Models-adjacent). IAS 16 · para 18 IFRIC 1 Illustration: 3.4.1 — Chemical-plant dismantling provision capitalised at construction KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.2 · Example 4 IAS 16 · para 16(c) IAS 37 · IE Example 3 PwC MOA 2020 Ch22 · FAQ 22.26.2 Facts Company H constructs a chemical plant with a useful life of 30 years. Environmental laws require H to dismantle the plant at the end of its useful life. No environmental damage from future operations has yet occurred. Typical industries with similar obligations: oil and gas, mining, telecommunications masts, nuclear power, landfill restoration (PwC). Assessment Present obligation exists under environmental law to dismantle at end of life — recognise a provision under IAS 37. IAS 16 · para 16(c): include the initial estimate in the cost of the PPE item when the obligation is incurred on acquisition / construction. No present obligation for damage that has yet to occur from future operations — do not provide or capitalise that amount. Conclusion Recognise dismantling provision; capitalise the same amount into plant cost. Do not capitalise or provide for future operating contamination not yet caused. If installation / testing itself contaminates the ground, that restoration estimate is also capitalised (KPMG Example 5). What decommissioning amounts enter PPE cost at construction? Illustration: 3.4.2 — Asbestos removal obligation created by a later law DTT iGAAP 2022 A7 · 4.2.10-1 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.2 · Example 7 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.2 · 3.2.110.10 PwC MOA 2020 Ch22 · FAQ 22.28.2 IAS 16 · para 7 IAS 16 · para BC14 IAS 37 Facts Company X acquired a building in 20X1. Asbestos was used in its construction. During 20X5, legislation requires Company X either to remove the asbestos or to vacate the building. Company X obtains a reliable estimate of the removal cost. At acquisition, Company X did not face this removal obligation. KPMG’s parallel is a chemical plant completed with no decommissioning law, and a law two years later that requires dismantling at the end of useful life. Assessment Recognise an IAS 37 provision for the cost of removing the asbestos. Paragraph 16 includes an initial dismantling estimate when the obligation is incurred on acquisition, or from use other than producing inventories. BC14 confirms that paragraph 16 does not specifically address obligations the entity did not face when it acquired the item, such as a law change after acquisition. Test paragraph 7. The removal cost can be measured reliably. Economic benefits from the removal are probable, because otherwise the building would be vacated. Both limbs are met, so capitalise the estimated removal cost. PwC: when a decommissioning obligation becomes apparent later because of a change in legislation, costs that meet the IAS 37 recognition criteria are capitalised as part of the asset’s cost to the extent they relate to installation, construction or acquisition. Damage from using the asset to produce inventory is an inventory cost. KPMG applies the principles used for a change in an existing obligation. Capitalisation should not leave the carrying amount above recoverable amount. The legislation is also an impairment indicator: when it is enacted, estimate the recoverable amount of the building. Conclusion Capitalise the estimated removal cost into the building and recognise the IAS 37 provision, because paragraph 7 is met. Do not expense the initial estimate merely because the law came after acquisition. Estimate recoverable amount when the legislation is enacted. Do not capitalise an obligation that arises from producing inventories. That cost follows IAS 2. KPMG Example 7 reaches the same capitalise-into-cost conclusion for a new end-of-life dismantling law, subject to recoverable amount. When a later law requires asbestos removal, what is capitalised and what else is tested?"
