    "chapter": "Chapter 3 — Initial measurement",
    "href": "../ias-16/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-5",
    "text": "3.5 Proceeds before intended use (IAS 16.20A) Items may be produced while bringing an item of PPE to the location and condition necessary for it to be capable of operating in the manner intended by management (such as samples produced when testing whether the asset is functioning properly). An entity recognises the proceeds from selling any such items, and the cost of those items, in profit or loss in accordance with applicable Standards. The entity measures the cost of those items applying the measurement requirements of IAS 2. IAS 16 · para 20A DTT iGAAP 2022 A7 · 4.2.1.3A EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · section 4.2.1 PwC MOA 2020 Ch22 · FAQ 22.20.5 Current rule (post May 2020 amendment). Proceeds from selling items produced while bringing PPE to intended location/condition → profit or loss . Cost of those items → profit or loss , measured under IAS 2. Do not deduct net proceeds from the cost of the PPE asset. IAS 16 · para 20A DTT iGAAP 2022 A7 · 4.2.1.3A Why IAS 2 for cost (Board / EY). IAS 2 provides a framework for measuring cost without being overly prescriptive; the same measurement applies whether or not the sale is an output of ordinary activities. In EY’s view it is not appropriate to limit those costs to only incremental costs — IAS 2 cost includes purchase, conversion and other costs bringing inventories to present location and condition (including systematic allocation of fixed and variable production overheads). IAS 16 · para BC16I EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · section 4.2.1 IAS 2 · para 10–12 Testing costs still capitalisable. Para 17(e) testing costs remain part of PPE cost; only the sale proceeds / cost of items produced are routed through P&L under 20A. IAS 16 · para 17(e) IAS 16 · para 20A Disclosure (para 74A). If not presented separately in the statement of comprehensive income, disclose the amounts of proceeds and cost included in profit or loss in accordance with para 20A that relate to items produced that are not an output of the entity’s ordinary activities, and which line item(s) include such proceeds and cost. Full disclosure package → Chapter 7. IAS 16 · para 74A DTT iGAAP 2022 A7 · 4.2.1.3A Incidental operations are different (para 21). Operations connected with construction but not necessary to bring the item to location/condition (for example using a building site as a car park until construction starts) produce income and related expenses recognised in profit or loss in their respective classifications — they never entered PPE cost. Do not confuse with 20A testing-output sales. IAS 16 · para 21 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · section 4.2 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.2 · Example 8B Do not apply pre-amendment netting. Before the May 2020 amendments, IAS 16 · para 17(e) required testing costs to be capitalised after deducting net proceeds from selling items produced while bringing the asset to location/condition. That netting is prohibited under current para 20A. Pack extracts that still describe deduction of testing proceeds (notably KPMG Insights 2019/20 · Example 8A; DTT pre-adoption 4.2.1.3B) are historical — teach the current P&L path only. IAS 16 · para 20A DTT iGAAP 2022 A7 · 4.2.1.3A See also — IAS 2. Cost of items produced under para 20A is measured applying IAS 2. IAS 16 · para 20A IAS 2 Illustration: 3.5.1 — Proceeds before intended use: P&L path under IAS 16.20A IAS 16 · para 20A IAS 16 · para 17(e) IAS 16 · para 20 IAS 16 · para 74A DTT iGAAP 2022 A7 · 4.2.1.3A EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · section 4.2.1 PwC MOA 2020 Ch22 · FAQ 22.20.5 Facts An entity is bringing an item of PPE to the location and condition necessary for it to be capable of operating in the manner intended by management. While doing so, it produces items that it can sell — Official example: samples produced when testing whether the asset is functioning properly (IAS 16 · para 20A). Testing activities assess technical and physical performance so the asset is capable of being used in production, supply, rental or administration (IAS 16 · para 17(e)). Some of the output may be imperfect / unfit for ordinary sale; some may be sold. The asset is not yet in the location and condition necessary to be capable of operating as intended — capitalisation of directly attributable costs (including testing costs) has not yet ceased (IAS 16 · para 20). Assessment — current rule Proceeds from selling any such items → recognise in profit or loss under applicable Standards (for example IFRS 15 when the sale is of goods). Cost of those items → recognise in profit or loss ; measure that cost applying IAS 2 (purchase, conversion and other costs to present location/condition — not an “incremental only” shortcut per EY). PPE cost still includes directly attributable testing costs under para 17(e), but without deducting net sale proceeds. Once the item is capable of operating as intended, stop capitalising further costs (para 20); subsequent output is ordinary operating activity, not 20A. If the produced items are not an output of ordinary activities and amounts are not presented separately, disclose proceeds and cost amounts and the line item(s) under para 74A. Conclusion — P&L paths Sale proceeds of testing / pre-intended-use output → P&L (not a reduction of PPE cost). IAS 2 cost of that output → P&L. Directly attributable testing costs → remain in PPE cost (para 17(e)). Do not net proceeds against PPE cost (pre-amendment practice retired). Contrast: incidental car-park / temporary rental income under para 21 was never a PPE-cost deduction either — it is a separate P&L classification (see Illustration 3.5.2 ). Item Where recognised Measurement / cite Proceeds from selling items produced while bringing PPE to intended use Profit or loss IAS 16 · para 20A Cost of those items Profit or loss IAS 2 measurement · IAS 16 · para 20A Costs of testing whether the asset is functioning properly PPE cost (until capable of operating as intended) IAS 16 · para 17(e) Net proceeds deducted from PPE cost Not permitted under current IFRS IAS 16 · para 20A Disclosure if not ordinary-activity output and not separate on SCI Notes (proceeds, cost, line item(s)) IAS 16 · para 74A Where do pre-intended-use sale proceeds and the related production costs go under current IAS 16? Illustration: 3.5.2 — Incidental operations: car park / temporary rental income in P&L IAS 16 · para 21 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.2 · Example 8B EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · section 4.2 DTT iGAAP 2022 A7 · 4.2.5 Facts Official example. Income is earned by using a building site as a car park until construction starts. KPMG Example 8B. Company K acquires a sports hall intending to construct a supermarket on the site. While waiting for construction permits, K rents the sports facilities to a local school. These activities are connected with the construction / development project but are not necessary to bring the eventual PPE item to the location and condition necessary for intended use. Assessment IAS 16 · para 21: incidental operations are not necessary to bring the item to location/condition for intended use. Income and related expenses of incidental operations are recognised in profit or loss in their respective classifications of income and expense. They are not included in determining the cost of the asset. This path is distinct from para 20A (sale of items produced while testing / bringing the asset itself to condition). Conclusion Recognise car-park / sports-rental income and related expenses in P&L. Do not deduct that income from PPE cost under construction. Keep 20A testing-output sales and para 21 incidental income in separate analytical buckets. How is income from temporary use of a construction site recognised?"
