    "chapter": "Chapter 3 — Initial measurement",
    "href": "../ias-16/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-7",
    "text": "3.7 Cash-price equivalent / deferred payment The cost of an item of PPE is the cash price equivalent at the recognition date. If payment is deferred beyond normal credit terms, the difference between the cash price equivalent and the total payment is recognised as interest over the period of credit unless such interest is capitalised in accordance with IAS 23. IAS 16 · para 23 PwC MOA 2020 Ch22 · 22.34 DTT iGAAP 2022 A7 · 4.3.2 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · section 4.1.6 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.2 · 3.2.130.10 Cash price equivalent, not IFRS 9 market-rate discounting by default (KPMG). IAS 16 specifically addresses deferred payment for PPE: cost is the current cash price at recognition. That calculation may differ from IFRS 9’s market-rate discounting of the financial liability; apply IAS 16 for the asset’s cost. Interest difference is recognised over the credit period (effective interest method in KPMG’s view) unless capitalised under IAS 23. KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.2 · 3.2.130.10 IAS 16 · para 23 “Normal credit terms” (DTT). Settlement that takes a few days, weeks or even months depending on industry and national law does not require interest imputation. A multi-year interest-free period advertised as an inducement is not normal credit terms — impute interest so the asset is recognised at current cash sale price. DTT iGAAP 2022 A7 · 4.3.2-2 Large up-front deposit (DTT). Where a non-refundable deposit is paid years before delivery and the total payable is reduced for time value, unwind the deposit’s time value into the cost of the asset using the rate implicit in the transaction. DTT iGAAP 2022 A7 · 4.3.2-3 IAS 16 · para 23 Illustration: 3.7.1 — Deferred payment: recognise asset at cash-price equivalent DTT iGAAP 2022 A7 · 4.3.2-1 IAS 16 · para 23 Facts On 1 January 20X1 an item of property is offered for sale at CU10 million. Payment terms: three equal instalments of CU3,333,333 over a two-year period (1 Jan 20X1, 31 Dec 20X1, 31 Dec 20X2). Developer offers a 5% discount (CU500,000) if payment is made in full at completion of the sale and purchase agreement — implicit interest rate 5.36% per annum. Purchaser takes up the deferred payment terms. Measurement Cash-price equivalent = CU10m − CU0.5m discount = CU9,500,000 . Initial recognition: Dr PPE 9,500,000; Cr Cash 3,333,333; Cr Accounts payable 6,166,667. End 20X1: interest expense 5.36% × 6,166,667 = CU330,459; pay second instalment (Dr interest 330,459; Dr payable 3,002,874; Cr cash 3,333,333). End 20X2: interest on remaining payable CU169,541; settle final instalment. Conclusion PPE cost = cash-price equivalent (CU9.5m), not the undiscounted sum of instalments (CU10m). Difference = interest over the credit period (unless IAS 23 capitalisation applies). Total cash paid still equals CU10m; interest is financing, not asset cost. How is PPE measured when settlement is deferred beyond normal credit terms?"
