    "chapter": "Chapter 3 — Initial measurement",
    "href": "../ias-16/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-7a",
    "text": "3.7A Variable / contingent consideration on PPE purchase A purchase of PPE may be an initial payment plus further amounts that are contingent on a future event, outcome, or sale of the acquired asset at a threshold price. IAS 16 does not choose a single method. Two approaches are observed when the future payment relates to the cost of the asset. Both are acceptable. The policy is applied consistently to similar transactions and disclosed when material. PwC MOA 2020 Ch22 · 22.32 PwC MOA 2020 Ch22 · 22.33 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · section 4.1.9 What the contingency can depend on. The buyer is usually obliged, by contract or by statute, to pay the additional amount if the future event or condition occurs. EY’s examples of variability are an index or a rate, the purchaser’s future actions, the condition of the asset, and the asset’s performance. One contract may contain more than one source. Arrangements that are akin to profit-sharing may sit outside any Standard. PwC MOA 2020 Ch22 · 22.32 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · section 4.1.9 First test: is the future payment related to the cost of the asset? Only after that analysis does the choice of approach arise. A payment that is not a cost of the asset is not forced into PPE. PwC MOA 2020 Ch22 · 22.33 IFRS Interpretations Committee — too broad for one answer. The IFRS IC discussed variable payments for an asset purchase between 2011 and 2016, including accounting at the purchase date and subsequently, and it distinguished payments that depend on the purchaser’s future activity from those that do not. The agenda decision (March 2016) determined that the issue was too broad to address within existing Standards and recommended that the IASB address variable payments comprehensively. No Standard selects one of the two approaches below. PwC MOA 2020 Ch22 · 22.32 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · section 4.1.9 When the liability is recognised is itself a policy. Read the contract: whether the payments are linked to the initial value of the asset or to its future performance; whether the contingency is inside or outside the purchaser’s control; and whether the variable amount is payment for some other good or service from the seller. Some contracts establish the obligation at the outset. Others are executory, and the liability is recognised only when a party performs. Where the contingency is outside the control of both parties, IAS 32 · para 25 treats a contingent obligation to deliver cash as a financial liability. EY records that this reading was discussed by the IFRS IC and was not confirmed in the final agenda decision. Develop the policy under the IAS 8 hierarchy so the information is relevant and reliable. Dissimilar payments may use different policies. Disclose the policy when it is material. EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · section 4.1.9 IAS 32 · para 25 Subsequent changes. How a later change in a recognised contingent liability is capitalised or expensed is worked in Illustration 3.7A.2 . EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · section 4.1.9 PwC MOA 2020 Ch22 · 22.33 Sources differ — contingent consideration related to the cost of PPE Source Treatment Official Paragraphs 15 and 16 measure an item of PPE at cost, and paragraph 23 sets the cash-price equivalent when payment is deferred beyond normal credit terms. Those paragraphs do not choose between excluding contingent purchase amounts from initial cost and recognising them with a day-one liability. IAS 16 · para 15 IAS 16 · para 16 IAS 16 · para 23 PwC If the future payment relates to the cost of the asset, two approaches are observed. Cost accumulation: contingent consideration is not included on initial recognition; it is added to the cost initially recorded when incurred, or when a related liability is remeasured for changes in cash flows. Financial-liability model: estimated future amounts payable are recorded on initial recognition of the asset, with a corresponding liability; remeasurements and additional payments are recognised in profit or loss or capitalised. Cost accumulation is more common. Both approaches are acceptable. Apply one policy consistently to all similar transactions and disclose it appropriately. PwC MOA 2020 Ch22 · 22.32 PwC MOA 2020 Ch22 · 22.33 EY Initial-recognition policies an entity may consider: include the fair value of all variable or contingent consideration at the acquisition date and recognise a matching liability; or exclude variable or contingent payments from initial cost and recognise no liability for them. Subsequent changes to a recognised liability are included in asset cost only to the extent they relate to future economic benefits embodied in the asset initially received (for example quality or quantity). Variability from use over time is generally expensed. Apply the IAS 8 hierarchy, consistently for similar transactions, and disclose the policy when material. EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · section 4.1.9 Shown side by side and not reconciled. See also — IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. Where the contingency is outside the control of both the issuer and the holder, IAS 32 · para 25 treats a contingent obligation to deliver cash as a financial liability. EY records that this pointer was discussed by the IFRS IC and was not confirmed in the final agenda decision. Measurement of embedded derivatives and of the time value of money under IFRS 9 is not developed here. IAS 32 · para 25 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · section 4.1.9 Illustration: 3.7A.2 — Subsequent changes: capitalise only if linked to asset initially received EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · section 4.1.9 PwC MOA 2020 Ch22 · 22.33 Facts The entity has already recognised PPE under a policy that includes a liability for contingent or variable consideration. After that recognition, the estimate of the amount payable changes. One change reflects the quality or the quantity of the asset that was initially received. Another change reflects later use of the asset, or another factor that is not the quality or quantity of what was received. Exchange rates, time value of money, and embedded derivatives are identified separately so they are not forced through the PPE-cost policy. Assessment Capitalise a subsequent change in a recognised liability only to the extent it relates to future economic benefits embodied in the asset initially received. Quality or quantity of that asset is the example of a change that can be capitalised. The opening asset and liability were an accounting estimate, updated under IAS 8. EY cites paragraph 37. Variability that arises because the asset is used over time is generally expensed. Exchange-rate changes follow IAS 21 and IFRIC 22. Time value of money and embedded derivatives follow IFRS 9. Those paths are not worked here. Where the variability is none of those cases, develop an IAS 8 policy from the substance of the contingency, apply it consistently to similar transactions, and disclose it when material. Conclusion Do not capitalise every remeasurement of the contingent liability into PPE. Capitalise only the portion linked to future economic benefits in the asset initially received. Expense variability that reflects use over time. Keep the subsequent-change policy consistent with the initial approach in 3.7A (the two approaches sit in the differ table ). When is a later change in contingent consideration added to PPE cost, and when is it expensed? See also — IAS 21 and IFRS 9. Changes in a recognised contingent-consideration liability that come from exchange rates are accounted for under IAS 21 and IFRIC 22. Time value of money and embedded derivatives are accounted for under IFRS 9. Neither Standard is applied in the plates above. EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · section 4.1.9 IAS 21 IFRS 9"
