    "chapter": "Chapter 4 — Subsequent measurement — models",
    "href": "../ias-16/draft-v1-linked/ch04.html#s-4-4",
    "text": "4.4 How to account for a revaluation mechanically When an item of PPE is revalued, the carrying amount of that asset is adjusted to the revalued amount. At the date of the revaluation, the asset is treated in one of two ways: (a) adjust the gross carrying amount consistently with the revaluation of the carrying amount (observable market data or proportionate restatement), and adjust accumulated depreciation to the difference; or (b) eliminate accumulated depreciation against the gross carrying amount. The adjustment of accumulated depreciation forms part of the increase or decrease accounted for under paragraphs 39 and 40. IAS 16 · para 35 PwC MOA 2020 Ch22 · 22.74 DTT iGAAP 2022 A7 · 6.4 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · section 6.2 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.2 · 3.2.340 Two mechanical methods — same revaluation gain/loss. Elimination (net) method zeroes accumulated depreciation so gross = net = revalued amount. Gross (restatement) method restates gross cost and accumulated depreciation so net equals revalued amount. Whichever method is selected, the amount recognised as the revaluation increase or decrease is the same. IAS 16 · para 35 DTT iGAAP 2022 A7 · 6.4-1 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · Illustration 6-3–6-5 Gross method sub-variants. Restate gross by reference to observable market data (e.g. as-new fair value), or restate gross proportionately to the change in carrying amount (useful when an index / depreciated replacement cost path is used). IAS 16 · para 35(a) EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · Illustration 6-4 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · Illustration 6-5 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.2 · Example 19A Elimination is the more common approach in practice (KPMG). Same approach is used when fully depreciated assets are revalued. KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.2 · 3.2.340 Illustration: 4.4.1 — Three mechanical methods — same building, same CU10,000 gain EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · Illustration 6-3 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · Illustration 6-4 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch19 · Illustration 6-5 IAS 16 · para 35 Facts Building carrying amount CU40,000 = original cost CU70,000 less accumulated depreciation CU30,000. Fair value at revaluation date = CU50,000. Revaluation gain = CU10,000 in every method. Eliminate (para 35(b)) Dr Accumulated depreciation CU30,000. Cr Building CU20,000; Cr revaluation surplus (OCI) CU10,000. After: gross at valuation CU50,000; accum dep nil; NBV CU50,000. Gross — observable / proportionate Observable market data: restate gross to CU75,000; accum dep to CU25,000; NBV CU50,000. Dr accum dep CU5,000; Dr Building CU5,000; Cr surplus CU10,000. Proportionate (25% uplift): gross CU87,500; accum dep CU37,500; NBV CU50,000. Dr Building CU17,500; Cr accum dep CU7,500; Cr surplus CU10,000. Gain recognised remains CU10,000 under all three paths. Method Gross after Accum dep after NBV OCI surplus Eliminate CU50,000 — CU50,000 CU10,000 Gross — observable CU75,000 CU25,000 CU50,000 CU10,000 Gross — proportionate CU87,500 CU37,500 CU50,000 CU10,000 Illustration: 4.4.2 — Mid-life useful-life change then revaluation — gross vs eliminate DTT iGAAP 2022 A7 · 6.4-1 IAS 16 · para 35 PwC MOA 2020 Ch22 · FAQ 22.74.1 Facts Entity E acquires asset 1 Jan 20X1 for CU100,000; useful life 20 years; nil residual. Beginning of 20X3: remaining useful life re-estimated at 10 years. End of 20X5 before revaluation: gross CU100,000; accum dep CU37,000; carrying amount CU63,000. Fair value CU84,000; observable as-new gross CU150,000; remaining useful life unchanged. Gross alternatives (35(a)) Observable: after revaluation gross CU150,000; accum dep CU66,000; CA CU84,000. Dr PPE CU50,000; Cr accum dep CU29,000; Cr OCI surplus CU21,000. Proportionate: gross CU133,000 (100,000 × 84/63); accum dep CU49,000; CA CU84,000. Dr PPE CU33,000; Cr accum dep CU12,000; Cr OCI surplus CU21,000. Eliminate (35(b)) · Conclusion Eliminate accum dep CU37,000 against gross; after revaluation gross = net = CU84,000. Journal: Dr accum dep CU37,000; Cr PPE CU16,000; Cr OCI surplus CU21,000. Revaluation gain is CU21,000 under every alternative (84,000 − 63,000)."
