    "chapter": "Chapter 2 — Recognition",
    "href": "../ias-2/draft-v1-linked/ch02.html#s-2-0",
    "text": "2.0 Recognition of inventories An entity initially recognises inventory when it has control of the inventory, expects it to provide future economic benefits, and the cost of the inventory can be measured reliably; IAS 2 itself contains no specific guidance on the timing of recognition. Recognition starts with the control, benefits and reliable-cost tests, then timing when IAS 2 itself gives no date test, measurement at cost on initial recognition, and recognition as an expense under paragraphs 34–35. PwC MOA 2020 · 25.9 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.90 Initial recognition criteria. An entity should initially recognise inventory when: It has control of the inventory It expects the inventory to provide future economic benefits The cost of the inventory can be measured reliably Inventories comprise assets: Held for sale in the ordinary course of business In the process of production for such sale In the form of materials or supplies to be consumed in the production process or in the rendering of services The definition and its boundaries are in Chapter 1 · 1.1 . PwC MOA 2020 · 25.9 Conceptual Framework · para 4.3 Conceptual Framework · para 4.4 Conceptual Framework · para BC5.1 IAS 2 · para 6 Timing. IAS 2 does not include specific guidance on the timing of recognition of purchased inventories. In the absence of that guidance, entities generally refer to the revenue recognition requirements: inventory is recognised on the date on which the entity obtains control of it ( 2.2 ). KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.90 IFRS 15 · para 31 Measurement on initial recognition. Initial measurement of inventories is at cost. All costs of purchase, costs of conversion and other costs incurred in bringing the inventories to their present location and condition, assigned to items using the cost formulas ( Chapter 3 ) After initial recognition, inventories are measured at the lower of cost and net realisable value ( Chapter 4 ) IAS 2 · para 9 IAS 2 · para 10 IAS 2 · para 23–27 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.15 Recognition as an expense. The only IAS 2 paragraphs headed as recognition deal with when the carrying amount of inventories is recognised as an expense — paragraphs 34–35 — on sale, on write-down or loss, on reversal of a write-down, and when inventories are allocated to another asset ( Chapter 5 ). IAS 2 · para 34 IAS 2 · para 35"
