    "text": "2.1 Items recognised as inventories Items recognised as inventories. Inventories include: Goods purchased and held for resale — merchandise purchased by a retailer, or land and other property held for resale The materials, work in progress and finished goods of a manufacturer Items that do not meet the definition of property, plant and equipment, such as certain spare parts, are treated as inventory IAS 2 · para 8 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.10 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.11 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.14 Recognition questions arise for spare parts and servicing equipment, pipeline fill, and property. Spare parts and servicing equipment. Spare parts and servicing equipment that do not meet the definition of property, plant and equipment are treated as inventory; parts used over more than one period are property, plant and equipment. Spare parts and servicing equipment Treated as Do not meet the definition of property, plant and equipment Treated as inventory Used over more than one period Property, plant and equipment Worked cases (car manufacturer; aircraft charter operator): Illustration 1.1.3 . PwC MOA 2020 · 25.10 PwC MOA 2020 · FAQ 25.10.1 IAS 16 · para 8 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.30 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · Example 1 Pipeline fill. Pipeline fill is recognised at cost when acquired and classified as inventories (materials or supplies consumed in rendering transportation services), not property, plant and equipment: Illustration 1.1.1 . DTT A11 2022 · 2.1-2 Property. Property purchased for the specific purpose of resale is recognised as inventory. Property is transferred from investment property to inventories when the entity commences the property’s development with a view to sale; the cost of the inventory is then built under Chapter 3 . The requirements of IAS 2 apply in their entirety to property intended for sale in the ordinary course of business or in the process of construction or development for such sale — for example, property acquired exclusively with a view to subsequent disposal in the near future or for development and resale. Classification on initial recognition (inventory or investment property) and the business model: Chapter 1 · 1.1 . Investment property transferred to inventories Investment property Property is transferred from investment property Development with a view to sale commences The entity commences the property’s development with a view to sale Inventories Transferred to inventories; the cost of the inventory is then built under Chapter 3 When is property transferred from investment property into inventories? PwC MOA 2020 · 25.12 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.13 IAS 40 · para 9(a) IAS 40 · para 57(b) DTT A11 2022 · 3.1"
