    "chapter": "Chapter 3 — Initial measurement",
    "href": "../ias-2/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-1",
    "text": "3.1 Cost of inventories Cost comprises three components — purchase, conversion and other costs to bring inventories to their present location and condition — while costs to fulfil a contract that do not create inventories fall under another Standard. Three components The cost of inventories shall comprise all costs of purchase, costs of conversion and other costs incurred in bringing the inventories to their present location and condition. Cost of inventories = Costs of purchase + Costs of conversion + Other costs Other costs include attributable non-production overheads. Costs of purchase: 3.2 Costs of conversion: 3.3 and 3.4 Other costs: 3.5 Costs excluded and recognised as expenses when incurred: 3.6 IAS 2 · para 10 IAS 2 · para 15 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.18 DTT A11 2022 · 3.2.1 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.120 Costs to fulfil a contract that do not give rise to inventories — another Standard. Costs incurred to fulfil a contract with a customer that do not give rise to inventories (or to assets within the scope of another Standard — for example, IAS 16 or IAS 38) are accounted for in accordance with that Standard: Chapter 1 · 1.4 . IAS 2 · para 8 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.14 DTT A11 2022 · 3.2.5 Components of the cost of inventories — paragraphs 10 to 22. Each row is one requirement of IAS 2; the last two rows are the cost of harvested agricultural produce and the techniques that may stand in for cost. IAS 2 · para 10 IAS 2 · para 11 IAS 2 · para 12 IAS 2 · para 15 IAS 2 · para 16 IAS 2 · para 18 IAS 2 · para 20 IAS 2 · para 21 Component Comprises IAS 2 Section Costs of purchase Purchase price; import duties and other taxes (other than those subsequently recoverable from the taxing authorities); transport, handling and other costs directly attributable to the acquisition of finished goods, materials and services — less trade discounts, rebates and other similar items para 11 3.2 Costs of conversion Costs directly related to the units of production (for example, direct labour); a systematic allocation of fixed production overheads (based on normal capacity) and variable production overheads (based on actual use); allocation between joint products and to by-products paras 12–14 3.3 , 3.4 Other costs Only to the extent incurred in bringing the inventories to their present location and condition — for example, non-production overheads or the costs of designing products for specific customers; borrowing costs in the limited circumstances identified in IAS 23 paras 15, 17 3.5 , 3.7 Not in the cost of inventories Recognised as expenses in the period incurred: abnormal amounts of wasted materials, labour or other production costs; storage costs, unless necessary in the production process before a further production stage; administrative overheads that do not contribute to present location and condition; selling costs. The financing element of deferred settlement terms is interest expense over the period of the financing paras 16, 18 3.6 , 3.7 Agricultural produce harvested from biological assets Fair value less costs to sell at the point of harvest (IAS 41) is the cost of the inventories at that date para 20 3.8 Techniques for the measurement of cost Standard cost method or retail method — used for convenience if the results approximate cost paras 21–22 3.9"
