    "href": "../ias-2/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-3",
    "text": "3.3 Costs of conversion Fixed production overheads are allocated on the basis of the normal capacity of the production facilities. Conversion cost covers direct production costs plus allocated overheads, fixed production overheads, variable production overheads, direct labour including employment taxes and benefits, depreciation on revalued PPE, why direct and marginal costing are not acceptable under IAS 2, and decommissioning or restoration costs caused by production. IAS 2 · para 12 IAS 2 · para 13 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.20 DTT A11 2022 · 3.2.3 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.170 Costs of conversion — direct costs of the units of production plus allocated production overheads. The costs of conversion of inventories include costs directly related to the units of production, such as direct labour. They also include a systematic allocation of fixed and variable production overheads that are incurred in converting materials into finished goods. Costs of conversion = Costs directly related to the units of production + Fixed production overheads + Variable production overheads Costs specifically attributable to units of production include direct labour, direct expenses and sub-contracted work. Production or conversion costs include all direct costs such as labour, material and direct overheads, and an allocation of fixed and variable production overheads. IAS 2 · para 12 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.20 DTT A11 2022 · 3.2.3.1 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.170.10 Fixed production overheads Fixed production overheads are those indirect costs of production that remain relatively constant regardless of the volume of production, such as depreciation and maintenance of factory buildings, equipment and right-of-use assets used in the production process, and the cost of factory management and administration. They also include local taxes such as business rates, and amortisation of intangible assets such as software used in the production process. IAS 2 · para 12 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.20 DTT A11 2022 · 3.2.3.3 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.170.10 Variable production overheads Variable production overheads are those indirect costs of production that vary directly, or nearly directly, with the volume of production, such as indirect materials and indirect labour. They are allocated to each unit of production on the basis of the actual use of the production facilities. IAS 2 · para 12 IAS 2 · para 13 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.22 DTT A11 2022 · 3.2.3.3 Direct labour — employment taxes and benefits included; costs need not be external or incremental. Direct labour includes all related employment taxes and benefits and any share-based payment costs. Labour costs include taxes and employee benefit costs associated with labour that is involved directly in the production process, and the costs do not need to be external or incremental. The cost of wages of employees directly engaged in production is allocated to production activities on the basis of normal operating conditions. Labour costs that result from operating inefficiencies, such as abnormal idle capacity or abnormal rectification work, are not included in the inventory valuation. DTT A11 2022 · 3.2.3.1 DTT A11 2022 · 3.2.3.2 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.170.10 IAS 2 · para 13 Property, plant and equipment under the revaluation model — depreciation on revalued amounts. When an entity adopts the revaluation model for items of property, plant and equipment, it considers, for the purpose of inventory valuation, depreciation based on the revalued amounts of those items. DTT A11 2022 · 3.2.3.3 IAS 2 · para 12 Direct costing and marginal costing — not acceptable under IAS 2. It is sometimes argued that overheads should be excluded from the cost of inventories on the grounds of prudence; such an approach (sometimes called “direct costing”) is not acceptable, because IAS 2 requires a systematic allocation of production overheads. “Marginal costing” approaches — under which only costs that vary directly with the volume of output are included in the measurement of inventories, and costs that accrue on a time basis (such as some overheads) are excluded — are not acceptable either, because they do not result in the allocation of fixed production overheads required by para 12. Direct costing and marginal costing Direct costing or marginal costing? Direct costing — it is sometimes argued that overheads should be excluded from the cost of inventories on the grounds of prudence Marginal costing — only costs that vary directly with the volume of output are included in the measurement of inventories, and costs that accrue on a time basis (such as some overheads) are excluded Not acceptable IAS 2 requires a systematic allocation of production overheads. Direct costing is not acceptable. Marginal costing approaches are not acceptable either, because they do not result in the allocation of fixed production overheads required by para 12. May inventories be measured using direct costing or marginal costing? DTT A11 2022 · 3.2.3.1-1 DTT A11 2022 · 3.2.3.1-2 IAS 2 · para 12 EY IGAAP 2026 · Ch23 · section 3.1.2 Decommissioning and restoration costs caused by production — part of the cost of that inventory. Decommissioning and restoration costs incurred as a consequence of the production of inventory in a particular period are part of the cost of that inventory. Accordingly, the effect of any changes to an existing obligation for decommissioning and restoration costs related to items that have been sold is recognised in profit or loss. KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.180.10 IAS 16 · para 16(c) IFRIC 1 · para 4"
