    "chapter": "Chapter 3 — Initial measurement",
    "href": "../ias-2/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-3-leases",
    "text": "Right-of-use assets and lease costs in the production of inventory Illustration: 3.3.6 — Right-of-use asset on leasehold land used in developing property for sale DTT A11 2022 · 3.2.3.3-3 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · Example 6A KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.205 IAS 2 · para 12 IFRS 16 · para 47 Facts An entity develops properties for sale that qualify as inventory within the scope of IAS 2; the properties are located on leasehold land . Example 6A: Construction Company P leases land to develop a multi-unit condominium complex for sale and recognises a related right-of-use asset . The land would not meet the definition of investment property, so P does not apply IAS 40 to the right-of-use asset. Depreciation of the right-of-use asset Depreciation of the leasehold land right-of-use asset during the construction period is incorporated in the cost of the property inventories, based on a reasonable allocation of that depreciation. Consistent with para 12: depreciation of right-of-use assets used in the production process is a fixed production overhead , allocated and included as part of the cost of the inventory. Presentation of the right-of-use asset The IFRS 16 presentation guidance for a lessee does not address an entity that sells a right-of-use asset in the course of its ordinary activities as part of inventory — for example, land leased for a multi-unit building that is transferred to the buyer. Accounting policy , applied consistently: include the right-of-use asset directly in the cost of inventory, or present it separately in the statement of financial position. If the right-of-use asset is presented separately, any related amortisation is capitalised as part of inventory . Illustration: 3.3.7 — Short-term lease of equipment used in construction: lease costs in the cost of inventory KPMG Insights 2019/20 · Example 6B KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.208 IFRS 16 · paras 5–6 Facts Modifying Illustration 3.3.6 (Example 6A): Construction Company P leases equipment to use in the construction of the multi-unit condominium complex. P determines that the equipment leases qualify for the short-term lease recognition exemption and elects to apply it. Lease costs There is no specific guidance on capitalising lease costs incurred in the production of inventory when an entity applies a recognition exemption to a short-term lease or a lease of a low-value item. If a recognition exemption is applied to a lease related to the production of inventory, the lease costs are included in the cost of inventory. P capitalises the lease payments related to the equipment leases directly into the cost of inventory . Contrast with Illustration 3.3.6 Right-of-use asset recognised (Example 6A): its depreciation is a fixed production overhead, and the asset is presented in inventory or separately by accounting policy ( Illustration 3.3.6 ). Recognition exemption applied (Example 6B): no right-of-use asset is recognised; the lease payments themselves enter the cost of inventory."
