    "href": "../ias-2/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-6",
    "text": "3.6 Costs excluded from the cost of inventories Abnormal wastage, storage costs (unless necessary in the production process before a further production stage), administrative overheads that do not contribute to bringing inventories to their present location and condition, and selling costs are excluded from the cost of inventories and recognised as expenses in the period in which they are incurred. Paragraph 16 lists costs excluded from inventory; impairment and general administration unrelated to production are also kept out; storage or holding costs are generally expensed with three exceptions; and cross-references show where the other exclusions are developed. IAS 2 · para 16 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.25 DTT A11 2022 · 3.2.4.2 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.170.20 Costs excluded from the cost of inventories — the four examples in para 16. Examples of costs excluded from the cost of inventories and recognised as expenses in the period in which they are incurred are: (a) abnormal amounts of wasted materials, labour or other production costs; (b) storage costs, unless those costs are necessary in the production process before a further production stage; (c) administrative overheads that do not contribute to bringing inventories to their present location and condition; and (d) selling costs. IAS 2 · para 16 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.25 DTT A11 2022 · 3.2.4.2 Impairment losses and general administration costs unrelated to production. Cost Allocated to the cost of inventory? Impairment losses (including goodwill impairment losses) Recognised as an expense and are not allocated to the cost of inventory Abnormal amounts of wasted material, labour or other production costs Recognised as an expense and are not allocated to the cost of inventory General administration costs unrelated to the production of inventory — for example, the costs of operating a finance department Recognised as an expense and are not allocated to the cost of inventory KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.170.20 IAS 2 · para 16 Storage or holding costs — generally expensed, with three exceptions. Storage or holding costs are generally expensed as they are incurred unless Storage is necessary before a further stage in the production process The inventory is produced as a discrete project (for example, custom-built furnishings, when the storage cost will be charged to the customer) Or the inventory requires a maturation process to bring it into a saleable condition (for example, whisky, wine or cheese) ( Illustration 3.6.1 ) KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.240 IAS 2 · para 16(b) Where the other exclusions are developed. Abnormal wastage: 3.2 Unallocated fixed overheads and operating inefficiencies: 3.3 Administrative overheads and general management: 3.3 Selling, distribution and transport costs: 3.5 Illustration: 3.6.1 — Storage costs during the ageing of whisky in cask before bottling PwC MOA 2020 · FAQ 25.25.3 IAS 2 · para 16(b) KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.240 EY IGAAP 2026 · Ch23 · section 3.1.3.A Facts The production of whisky involves the ageing of whisky in a cask prior to bottling. Question: can the storage costs be included in the cost of inventory? Storage before a further production stage Capitalisation of storage costs is allowed only if the storage is necessary in the production process prior to a further production stage (para 16(b)). Inventory that requires a maturation process to bring it into a saleable condition — for example, whisky, wine or cheese — is one of the exceptions to the general rule that storage or holding costs are expensed as incurred. Ageing costs capitalised The storage costs that the entity incurs during the ageing process are capitalised . Ageing is integral to making the finished product saleable. Borrowing costs during the maturing period: 3.7 ."
