    "chapter": "Chapter 3 — Initial measurement",
    "href": "../ias-2/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-ex-lifo",
    "text": "Prohibition of LIFO LIFO is not permitted; the Board eliminated it because it does not reliably represent inventory flows; arguments for keeping LIFO were considered and rejected; and specific cost methods similar to LIFO remain possible while tax LIFO does not dictate the IFRS formula. LIFO is not permitted. The Standard does not permit the use of the last-in, first-out (LIFO) formula to measure the cost of inventories. The previous version of IAS 2 and SIC-1 allowed some choice between FIFO or weighted average cost formulas (benchmark treatment) and the LIFO method (allowed alternative treatment); the Board decided to eliminate the allowed alternative of using LIFO. Para 25 permits only FIFO and the weighted average cost formula. IAS 2 · para IN13 IAS 2 · para BC9 IAS 2 · para 25 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.17 DTT A11 2022 · 3.2.8.1 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.280.30 EY IGAAP 2026 · Ch23 · section 3.2.2.C Why LIFO was eliminated — not a reliable representation of inventory flows. LIFO treats the newest items of inventory as sold first, so the items remaining are recognised as if they were the oldest — generally not a reliable representation of actual inventory flows. It attempts to meet a perceived deficiency of the conventional model (cost of goods sold measured at outdated prices while revenue is at current prices) by imposing an unrealistic cost flow assumption. Its use is often tax-driven; its effect on profits depends on the relationship between the prices of the most recent acquisitions and replacement cost at the end of the period, so it is not a truly systematic method for determining the effect of changing prices on profits. It results in inventories in the balance sheet at amounts that bear little relationship to recent cost levels, and can distort profit or loss, especially when “preserved” older layers are presumed to have been used when inventories are substantially reduced. IAS 2 · para BC10 IAS 2 · para BC11 IAS 2 · para BC12 IAS 2 · para BC13 Arguments for LIFO considered and rejected. Arguments for LIFO Rejected Partial adjustment of profit or loss for price changes Not appropriate to allow a measurement of profit or loss inconsistent with the measurement of inventories for balance sheet purposes Security stocks (for example, in the oil and gas industry, often a minimum of 90 days of sales) These do not match historical layers under a LIFO computation Coal dumps and piles of iron or metal scrap replenished by “topping up” Their valuation follows a direct costing approach matching actual physical flows with direct costs, which is different from LIFO IAS 2 · para BC14 IAS 2 · paras BC15–BC16 IAS 2 · paras BC17–BC18 Specific cost methods similar to LIFO, and tax considerations. LIFO was eliminated because of its lack of representational faithfulness of inventory flows; the decision does not rule out specific cost methods that reflect inventory flows similar to LIFO. Where LIFO for tax purposes is possible only if it is also used for accounting purposes, tax considerations do not provide an adequate conceptual basis for selecting an accounting treatment. IAS 2 · para BC19 IAS 2 · para BC20 Illustration: 3.10.1 — Applying cost formulas: FIFO and weighted-average cost methods (periodic inventory system) KPMG Insights 2019/20 · Example 7A KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.280 IAS 2 · para 25 IAS 2 · para 27 EY IGAAP 2026 · Ch23 · section 3.2.2 Facts — December 2019 An entity uses a periodic inventory system; opening inventory and purchases total 1,000 units at a cost of 12,250 . Closing inventory on hand: 400 units. The standard-cost variance allocation on the same facts: Illustration 3.9.3 . Units Unit cost Total cost Opening inventory 200 10 2,000 Purchases 50 11 550 Purchases 400 12 4,800 Purchases 350 14 4,900 Total 1,000 12,250 FIFO The 400 units remaining are those most recently purchased: 350 @ 14 and 50 @ 12. Cost of sales carries opening inventory and the earlier purchases: 2,000 + 550 + 350 @ 12 (4,200) = 6,750. Closing layers Amount 350 @ 14 4,900 50 @ 12 600 Closing inventory 5,500 Cost of sales (12,250 − 5,500) 6,750 Weighted average Weighted average of opening inventory and all purchases in the period: 12,250 / 1,000 = 12.25 per unit. Cost of sales: 600 units × 12.25 = 7,350. Step Amount Weighted average unit cost (12,250 / 1,000) 12.25 Closing inventory (400 × 12.25) 4,900 Cost of sales (12,250 − 4,900) 7,350 Illustration: 3.10.2 — Weighted average cost formula and FIFO cost formula: purchases during the year DTT A11 2022 · 3.2.8.4-1 DTT A11 2022 · 3.2.8.5-1 IAS 2 · para 27 EY IGAAP 2026 · Ch23 · section 3.2.2 Facts Opening inventories of 15,000 units at a weighted average cost of CU4 per unit (CU60,000). Purchases during the year of 75,000 units for CU322,000 ; with opening inventories, 90,000 units costing CU382,000 . Closing inventories are 20,000 units. Weighted average cost formula The number of units in closing inventories is multiplied by the weighted average cost per unit for the year. CU382,000 / 90,000 = CU4.24 per unit. Closing inventories: CU4.24 × 20,000 = CU84,800 . FIFO cost formula The first units held are the first units sold, so closing inventories are valued at the cost per unit of the latest purchases . October 5,000 @ CU4.50 (22,500) + July 10,000 @ CU4.40 (44,000) + May 5,000 @ CU4.10 (20,500). Closing inventories: CU87,000 . Date Units Cost per unit (CU) Total cost (CU) In FIFO closing inventories Opening inventories 15,000 4.00 60,000 — 1 January 15,000 4.20 63,000 — 1 April 20,000 4.50 90,000 — 1 May 25,000 4.10 102,500 5,000 units — CU20,500 1 July 10,000 4.40 44,000 10,000 units — CU44,000 1 October 5,000 4.50 22,500 5,000 units — CU22,500 Total 90,000 382,000 20,000 units — CU87,000 Illustration: 3.10.3 — Determining the cost of inventories using the FIFO and weighted average cost methods PwC MOA 2020 · FAQ 25.32.1 IAS 2 · para 27 Facts Opening inventory on 1 March 20X9 is nil ; all inventory is finished goods of the same type. Batch 1 received on 1 March 20X9: 2 units at C3.00 ; batch 2 received on 15 March 20X9: 4 units at C4.50 . On 25 March 20X9 the entity sells 5 units; closing inventory on 31 March 20X9 is 1 unit. FIFO All units of batch 1 are sent out first, then three units from batch 2. One unit from batch 2 remains at C4.50 . Weighted average The average unit cost of all units received is ((2 × C3) + (4 × C4.50)) / (2 + 4) = C4.00 . One unit remains at C4.00 . March 20X9 Units Unit cost (C) Value (C) Batch 1 received 1 March 2 3.00 6.00 Batch 2 received 15 March 4 4.50 18.00 Sold 25 March (5) Closing inventory 31 March — FIFO 1 4.50 4.50 Closing inventory 31 March — weighted average 1 4.00 4.00 Closing inventory in Illustrations 3.10.1 to 3.10.3 Units on hand FIFO Weighted average Weighted average unit cost 3.10.1 — periodic system, December 2019 400 5,500 4,900 12.25 3.10.2 — purchases during the year 20,000 CU87,000 CU84,800 CU4.24 3.10.3 — two batches in March 20X9 1 C4.50 C4.00 C4.00 Illustration: 3.10.4 — Cost formulas in different segments: refined gold KPMG Insights 2019/20 · Example 8 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.320 IAS 2 · para 25 IAS 2 · para 26 EY IGAAP 2026 · Ch23 · section 3.2.2 Facts Company J buys gold and refines it. Some of the refined gold is sold by the wholesale segment; the remainder is used by a segment that manufactures jewellery . Cost formulas in the two segments The wholesale and jewellery segments may use different cost formulas to account for the refined gold. Raw materials used by one segment may have a different use from the same raw materials used in another segment, which may justify a different treatment (para 26). Limits on different cost formulas The same cost formula is applied to all inventories having a similar nature and use to the entity (para 25). A difference in geographical location, by itself, does not justify different cost formulas (para 26). Minimum inventory levels and changes of cost formula: 3.10 ."
