    "chapter": "Chapter 4 — Subsequent measurement",
    "href": "../ias-2/draft-v1-linked/ch04.html#s-4-5-purpose",
    "text": "Purpose for which the inventory is held — intended use Estimated selling price follows the intended use of the inventory; operating losses alone do not trigger an automatic write-down. Intended use. The estimated selling price takes into account the intended use of the items. The calculation of NRV takes account of the intended use of the inventory — for example, the NRV of inventories held to satisfy a particular sales or service contract is based on the contract price ( firm sales contracts ). Materials held for use in production, and materials to be sold in their current form: 4.6 . KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.350.10 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.38 IAS 2 · para 31 Operating losses. Operating losses do not result in an automatic write-down of inventory ( Illustration 4.5.7 ). KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.350.30 Illustration: 4.5.6 — Raw material timber and undeveloped land: NRV based on the intended use KPMG Insights 2019/20 · Example 11A KPMG Insights 2019/20 · Example 11B KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.350.25–29 IAS 2 · para 31 IAS 2 · para 32 Example 11A — timber held to manufacture cabinets Company P, a cabinet manufacturer, has raw material timber inventory at 31 December 2019 with a carrying amount of 100 ; the current market value of that timber is 95 . P intends to use the timber to manufacture cabinets, so NRV is based on the finished cabinets and not on the timber in its raw material form: selling price of the cabinets 160 ; estimated costs to completion and sale 50 . NRV of the timber is higher than its carrying amount of 100 — no write-down at 31 December 2019. Example 11B — land held to develop houses Company Q, a property developer, has undeveloped land inventory at 31 December 2019 with a carrying amount of 1,000 ; the current market value of that undeveloped land is 950 . Q intends to develop houses on the land, so NRV is based on the estimated selling prices of the finished houses less the estimated costs of completion and sale. The land is not written down at 31 December 2019 if Q estimates that the finished houses will be sold above cost. Sale in the current form If P intended to sell the timber in its current raw material form, it would write down the timber by 5 plus the estimated costs of sale . If Q intended to sell the land in its current form, it would write down the land by 50 plus the estimated costs of sale . The estimated selling price takes into account the intended use of the items (para 31). NRV of the timber 110 = Selling price of the cabinets 160 − Estimated costs to completion and sale 50 Inventory at 31 December 2019 Carrying amount Current market value Intended use NRV based on Write-down Timber (Company P) 100 95 Manufacture cabinets Finished cabinets: 160 − 50 = 110 None Timber (Company P) 100 95 Sale in its raw material form Timber in its current form 5 plus estimated costs of sale Undeveloped land (Company Q) 1,000 950 Develop houses Finished houses, less estimated costs of completion and sale None, if the finished houses will be sold above cost Undeveloped land (Company Q) 1,000 950 Sale in its current form Land in its current form 50 plus estimated costs of sale Illustration: 4.5.7 — Operating losses in a servicing division: NRV of parts by intended use KPMG Insights 2019/20 · Example 12 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.350.30–50 IAS 2 · para 31 Facts Company T is a tractor producer and also has a servicing division that is operating at a loss. T has some parts that will be used in the servicing business and others that will be used in the production of tractors. NRV of the parts Operating losses do not result in an automatic write-down of inventory. T evaluates the NRV of the parts to be used in tractors separately from the NRV of the parts to be used in the servicing division. Conclusion The losses in the servicing division may result in a write-down of the parts that will be used by that division . They do not result in a write-down of the parts that will be used in production . Parts to be used in NRV evaluated Effect of the servicing division’s losses Servicing division (operating at a loss) Separately May result in a write-down Production of tractors Separately No write-down"
