    "chapter": "Chapter 4 — Subsequent measurement",
    "href": "../ias-2/draft-v1-linked/ch04.html#s-4-5-contracts",
    "text": "Firm sales contracts Firm sales contracts set the NRV selling price for inventory held to satisfy them; sales-commitment derivatives in IFRS 9 interact with that measurement; inventory is written down when the cost of fulfilling a sales contract exceeds the contract price; and onerous future purchase or sale contracts may need provisions. Firm sales contracts. The determination of NRV takes into account firm sales contracts that have been entered into. The NRV of inventory held to satisfy firm sales contracts is based on the selling price under those contracts The NRV of inventory held in excess of firm sales contracts is based on general selling prices without regard to the firm sales contracts KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.390.10 IAS 2 · para 31 DTT A11 2022 · 3.3.1 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.38 Sales commitment derivatives. Where forward contracts entered into to manage inventory levels are in the scope of IFRS 9 and accounted for under its guidance, in KPMG’s view the NRV of the inventory held to satisfy firm sales contracts is based on the contract price including the fair value of the related sales commitment derivatives; the NRV of inventory held in excess of firm sales contracts is based on general selling prices without regard to the specific sales contracts ( Illustration 4.5.10 ). KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.390.50 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.390.60 Cost of fulfilling. If an entity has a contract to sell inventory for less than the cost of fulfilling the obligations under the contract, the inventory held to satisfy that contract is written down by the amount by which the cost of fulfilling the obligations exceeds the selling price. If that excess is greater than the carrying amount of the inventory, the entity considers the guidance in IAS 37 on onerous contracts. KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.390.70 IAS 2 · para 31 Onerous contracts. Contracts for future sales, or for future purchases of raw materials, might give rise to provisions where such contracts are onerous — for example, a future sales contract set at a price below the entity’s cost of production of the relevant goods, or a purchase commitment for goods or raw materials at a price above the price at which the entity can sell on the goods or manufacture the finished product from the raw materials. Onerous contracts are accounted for under IAS 37. PwC MOA 2020 · 25.38 IAS 2 · para 31 Inventory quantity or contract Net realisable value or accounting Source Held to satisfy firm sales or service contracts Contract price IAS 2 · para 31 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.390.10 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.38 DTT A11 2022 · 3.3.1 Held to satisfy firm sales contracts, with related sales commitment derivatives in the scope of IFRS 9 Contract price including the fair value of the related sales commitment derivatives KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.390.60 Held in excess of firm sales contracts General selling prices, without regard to the firm sales contracts IAS 2 · para 31 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.390.10 DTT A11 2022 · 3.3.1 Held to satisfy a contract to sell for less than the cost of fulfilling it Written down by the amount by which the cost of fulfilling the obligations exceeds the selling price KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.390.70 That excess greater than the carrying amount of the inventory; firm sales contracts in excess of inventory quantities held; onerous firm purchase contracts Provisions — IAS 37 (onerous contracts) IAS 2 · para 31 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.38 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.390.70 Illustration: 4.5.8 — Printers held partly to meet a firm sales contract DTT A11 2022 · 3.3.1-4 IAS 2 · para 31 EY IGAAP 2026 · Ch23 · section 3.3.1 Facts Entity A, a supplier of office equipment, holds 1,000 printers at the reporting date with a cost of CU150 per unit. In the past these printers sold for CU200 per unit. Because of recent changes in technology and the imminent arrival of a new model, they could only be sold in the market for CU130 per unit at the reporting date. Before the year end, A secured a firm sales contract with a local education authority to supply 500 printers at CU170 per unit, to be fulfilled by delivery in the next reporting period. Selling costs are nil. Printers for the firm sales contract NRV is CU170 per unit, which is higher than cost. These units continue to be measured at their cost of CU150 per unit: 500 × CU150 = CU75,000 . Remaining printers NRV reflects the current market price of CU130 per unit, which is lower than cost. A writes these printers down by CU10,000 (500 units × CU20) to their NRV of CU65,000 (500 units × CU130). Total carrying amount of printer inventories: CU140,000 . Entity A — printers Units NRV per unit Cost per unit Carrying amount Write-down To be supplied under the firm sales contract 500 CU170 (contract price) CU150 CU75,000 — Remaining printers 500 CU130 (market price) CU150 CU65,000 CU10,000 Total 1,000 CU140,000 CU10,000 Illustration: 4.5.9 — Firm sales contract for part of the units on hand KPMG Insights 2019/20 · Example 15 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.390.20–40 IAS 2 · para 31 Facts Company P has 100 units of inventory on hand at its annual reporting date, recorded at a carrying amount of 10 per unit. The current market price is 8 per unit. P has a firm sales contract with Company Q to sell 60 units at 11 per unit, which cannot be settled net. 60 units to be sold to Company Q NRV is 11 per unit less estimated selling costs — the selling price under the firm sales contract. Remaining 40 units NRV is 8 per unit less estimated selling costs — the general selling price. P writes down the 40 units by 80 (40 × (10 − 8)) plus estimated selling costs. Company P — units NRV per unit Carrying amount per unit Write-down 60 — firm sales contract with Company Q 11 less estimated selling costs 10 — 40 — remaining units 8 less estimated selling costs 10 80 (40 × (10 − 8)) plus estimated selling costs Illustration: 4.5.10 — Firm sales contracts with sales commitment derivatives KPMG Insights 2019/20 · Example 16 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.390.50–60 IAS 2 · para 31 Facts Company E manufactures semi-finished copper and copper alloy products. E enters into forward contracts to manage its copper inventory level. Those forward contracts fall in the scope of IFRS 9 and are accounted for under its guidance. Inventory held to satisfy firm sales contracts NRV is based on the contract price including the fair value of the related sales commitment derivatives. Inventory in excess of firm sales contracts NRV is based on the general selling prices , without regard to the specific sales contracts. The same split without derivatives: Illustration 4.5.8 and Illustration 4.5.9 ."
