    "chapter": "Chapter 4 — Subsequent measurement",
    "href": "../ias-2/draft-v1-linked/ch04.html#s-4-6",
    "text": "4.6 Materials and other supplies held for use in production Materials held for production are not written down if the finished products will realise at least cost; NRV then follows the finished products’ estimated selling price unless the materials will be sold in their current form; excess materials that will be sold are measured on their own anticipated sale; lower replacement cost does not by itself force a write-down; and parts and sub-assemblies may need review when finished goods are written down. Finished products. Written down only when the cost of the finished products exceeds NRV. Materials and other supplies held for use in the production of inventories are not written down below cost if the finished products in which they will be incorporated are expected to be sold at or above cost. However, when a decline in the price of materials indicates that the cost of the finished products exceeds net realisable value, the materials are written down to net realisable value. In such circumstances, the replacement cost of the materials may be the best available measure of their net realisable value. A write-down is not necessary where the NRV of material inventories is less than the purchase price, provided that the finished goods into which the materials are to be incorporated can still be sold at a profit after incorporating the materials at cost price. IAS 2 · para 32 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.40 DTT A11 2022 · 3.3.2 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.350.25 Current form. Case Treatment Held for use in the production of inventories If materials and other supplies are held for use in the production of inventories, NRV is based on the estimated selling price of the finished products in which they will be incorporated (less the estimated costs of completion and sale). Sold in their current form If the entity intends to sell materials in their current form rather than incorporate them into finished products, NRV is based on the estimated selling price of the materials in their current form. NRV based on the finished products — or on the materials in their current form if they are to be sold. Illustration 4.5.6 . KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.350.25 Excess materials. Company B has excess inventories of materials that it will not be able to use in production, and has decided to sell them; the NRV of the excess materials is based on their anticipated sale. KPMG Insights 2019/20 · Example 10 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.350.20 IAS 2 · para 32 Replacement cost. Lower replacement costs — no automatic write-down. Lower replacement costs do not lead automatically to an NRV write-down; a write-down is recognised if the fall in prices means that the finished products will be sold for less than production cost. KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.370.10 IAS 2 · para 32 Parts and sub-assemblies. Where a write-down is required to reduce the value of finished goods below cost, the stocks of the parts and sub-assemblies held for the purpose of the manufacture of such products, together with inventories on order, might need to be reviewed to determine whether a write-down is also required against such items. PwC MOA 2020 · 25.40 Materials and other supplies held for use in production — paragraph 32 Decline in the price of materials Lower replacement costs do not lead automatically to a write-down Are the finished products expected to be sold at or above cost? NRV of the materials is based on the finished products in which they will be incorporated, less the estimated costs of completion and sale yes Materials not written down below cost no Materials written down to NRV Replacement cost may be the best available measure; parts, sub-assemblies and inventories on order reviewed Are materials and other supplies held for use in production written down below cost when the finished products are expected to be sold at or above cost? Illustration: 4.6.1 — Semi-finished telecommunication equipment: NRV based on the finished product PwC MOA 2020 · FAQ 25.40.1 IAS 2 · para 32 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.350.25 EY IGAAP 2026 · Ch23 · section 3.3.1.A Facts An entity manufactures telecommunication equipment in three stages . There is a market for the semi-finished product at each stage, but the entity only sells the completed product . Cost structure per unit at 31 December 20X2 (financial year end): see the table below. Selling costs are immaterial . Question: what is the NRV of the semi-finished product in stage 1 at 31 December 20X2? Assessment Although the selling price per unit at stage 1 is C120 , the calculation of the NRV of work in progress considers the expected selling price of the finished products in which it will be incorporated (para 32). The profit margin on the estimated cost of completion is therefore considered when calculating the NRV of work in progress, if the entity has the ability to dispose of the finished product at a price that exceeds the production cost . Conclusion NRV of the semi-finished product at stage 1: selling price of the completed product, less stage 3 conversion costs, less stage 2 conversion costs. Cost structure at 31 December 20X2 Cost per unit (C) Selling price per unit (C) Stage 1 150 120 Stage 2 — conversion costs 40 90 After stage 2 190 210 Stage 3 — conversion costs 60 70 Completed product 250 280 Net realisable value at stage 1 180 = Selling price of the completed product 280 − Stage 3 conversion costs (60) − Stage 2 conversion costs (40)"
