    "chapter": "Chapter 4 — Subsequent measurement",
    "href": "../ias-2/draft-v1-linked/ch04.html#s-4-7",
    "text": "4.7 Reversal of write-downs A new assessment of NRV is made in each subsequent period; a write-down is reversed when the circumstances that caused it no longer exist or there is clear evidence of an increase in NRV — limited to the amount of the original write-down. A write-down is reversed when a new assessment shows NRV has recovered, in whole or in part, with the reversal recognised in profit or loss, while items still on hand after a long period may still raise obsolescence questions. IAS 2 · para 33 New assessment. A new assessment is made of net realisable value in each subsequent period. When the circumstances that previously caused inventories to be written down below cost no longer exist or when there is clear evidence of an increase in net realisable value because of changed economic circumstances, the amount of the write-down is reversed (ie the reversal is limited to the amount of the original write-down) so that the new carrying amount is the lower of the cost and the revised net realisable value. This occurs, for example, when an item of inventory that is carried at net realisable value, because its selling price has declined, is still on hand in a subsequent period and its selling price has increased. The Standard describes the circumstances that would trigger a reversal of a write-down of inventories recognised in a prior period. IAS 2 · para 33 IAS 2 · para IN15 DTT A11 2022 · 3.3.3 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.41 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.335.20 EY IGAAP 2026 · Ch23 · section 3.3.1.C All or part. If the circumstances that caused the write-down cease to exist, such that all or part of the write-down is no longer needed, it is reversed to that extent. PwC MOA 2020 · 25.41 Profit or loss. The amount of the write-down is reversed through profit or loss so that the new carrying amount is the lower of the cost and the revised NRV. Reversals are recognised in profit or loss in the period in which the reversal occurs, as a reduction in the amount of inventories recognised as an expense in the period ( Chapter 5 · 5.3 ). DTT A11 2022 · 3.3.3 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.335.20 IAS 2 · para 34 Obsolescence. When an item carried at NRV because its selling price had declined is still on hand in a subsequent period and its selling price has increased, it would still be necessary, if the item had been on hand for a long time, to consider whether there might be obsolescence issues. DTT A11 2022 · 3.3.3 Write-down and reversal — paragraphs 33 and 34 Period of the write-down Cost not recoverable: inventories written down to NRV; the write-down is recognised as an expense in that period Each subsequent period A new assessment of NRV is made Circumstances no longer exist, or clear evidence of an increase in NRV Reversal — limited to the amount of the original write-down New carrying amount — lower of cost and revised NRV Profit or loss — reduction in the amount of inventories recognised as an expense When the circumstances that caused a write-down no longer exist, is the reversal limited to the amount of the original write-down? Illustration: 4.7.1 — Reversal of a write-down limited to the original write-down KPMG Insights 2019/20 · Example 9 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.335.30–40 IAS 2 · para 33 EY IGAAP 2026 · Ch23 · section 3.3.1.C Facts On 31 December 2018 , Company P writes down its inventory from a carrying amount of 100 to its NRV of 95 . During 2019 , the inventory is still on hand and its NRV increases to 103 . New assessment of NRV in 2019 A new assessment of NRV is made in each subsequent period; the revised NRV is 103 (para 33). The reversal is limited to the amount of the original write-down, so that the new carrying amount is the lower of cost and the revised NRV. Conclusion P recognises a reversal of 5 , so that the new carrying amount of the inventory is the lower of its cost of 100 and its revised NRV of 103 — 100 . The reversal is recognised in profit or loss as a reduction in the amount of inventories recognised as an expense in 2019 (para 34; Chapter 5 · 5.3 ). Company P Cost NRV Carrying amount Recognised in profit or loss 31 December 2018 100 95 95 Write-down from 100 to 95 2019 — inventory still on hand 100 103 100 Reversal of 5 — limited to the original write-down Illustration: 4.7.2 — Accounting policy for inventories: cost formula, net realisable value and materials EY IGAAP 2026 · Ch23 · Practical example 3-2 IAS 2 · para 9 IAS 2 · para 25 IAS 2 · para 30 IAS 2 · para 32 IAS 2 · para 33 Accounting policy disclosed Building-materials group (CRH plc 2022) states inventories at the lower of cost and NRV . Cost uses FIFO , with weighted average where appropriate — same-nature / same-use consistency. Cost includes Expenditure to acquire and bring inventories to present location and condition Finished goods and work in progress include direct materials, direct labour and attributable overheads on normal operating capacity Borrowing costs excluded NRV = estimated proceeds of sale less costs to complete and costs of marketing, selling and distribution. Net realisable value Estimates consider price fluctuations after the period end only to the extent they confirm period-end conditions (para 30). Also weigh buyer preferences, obsolescence and perishability when assessing recoverability. Materials for production are not written down below cost if the finished goods they enter are expected to be sold at or above cost (para 32). Climate-related and policy risks are flagged as a future valuation consideration; short inventory turnover meant no material climate-related write-downs in that year. Conclusion The policy brings together the lower of cost and NRV, the cost formula, what is included in cost, how NRV is estimated and the materials rule. Write-downs are reassessed in each subsequent period and reversed in the circumstances in paragraph 33 ( 4.7 )."
