    "text": "6.5 Analysis of expenses by function or by nature Under a nature-of-expense format, entities disclose amounts other than cost of sales; two approaches satisfy the requirement to disclose inventories recognised as an expense. Nature of expense Some entities adopt a format for profit or loss that results in amounts being disclosed other than the cost of inventories recognised as an expense during the period. Under this format, an entity presents an analysis of expenses using a classification based on the nature of expenses. In this case, the entity discloses the costs recognised as an expense for raw materials and consumables, labour costs and other costs together with the amount of the net change in inventories for the period. IAS 2 · para 39 PwC MOA 2020 · 25.49 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.400.60 Two ways to satisfy the requirement to disclose the amount of inventories recognised as an expense. Some entities adopt a classification of expenses according to the nature of the expense, and others present expenses based on their function. An entity that adopts the ‘nature of expense’ approach discloses wages of factory employees as staff costs; one that adopts the ‘function of expense’ approach discloses such wages as part of cost of sales. An entity that allocates expenses by function discloses the cost of inventories sold during the period ( 6.4 ). PwC MOA 2020 · 25.49 KPMG Insights 2019/20 · 3.8.400.60 IAS 2 · para 36(d) Analysis of expenses Inventory-related amounts disclosed Wages of factory employees disclosed as By function. IAS 2 · para 36(d) IAS 2 · para 38 Amount of inventories recognised as an expense in the period — within cost of sales Costs previously included in inventories Unallocated production overheads And abnormal amounts of production costs Part of cost of sales By nature. IAS 2 · para 39 Costs recognised as an expense for raw materials and consumables Labour costs And other costs Together with the net change in inventories for the period Staff costs"
