    "chapter": "Chapter 2 — PFS roles & aggregation",
    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch02.html#s-2-0",
    "text": "2.0 Scope — where information lives Start with location, not category mechanics. IFRS 18 gives the PFS and the notes distinct but complementary roles. The PFS provide useful structured summaries; the notes provide material detail and supplementation. Aggregation, disaggregation and faithful labelling are the controls that make those roles work. IFRS 18 · para 15–18 IFRS 18 · para 41–45 IFRS 18 · para B16–B26 Four decisions settle where information lives. The first asks whether the information is material, the second where it does its job, the third whether the grouping obscures, and the fourth whether the label tells users what is inside. First decision — is the information material? An entity need only present or disclose material information. A specific Standard requirement can be omitted when the resulting information is not material, but additional disclosure may still be needed when the specific requirements are insufficient for users to understand the effects of transactions, events or conditions. Materiality requirements apply equally to every presentation and disclosure requirement: they set out whether information is provided, not where in the financial statements it is provided. IFRS 18 · para 19–20 IFRS 18 · para BC53 KPMG FI 2024 · section 4.1 EY Closer look 2026 · section 2 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 2 Second decision — where does the information do its job? Present summarised information in the PFS when it contributes to an understandable overview, comparisons, or a cue to seek more information; disclose material detail in the notes when users need to understand a PFS line or when the information supplements the PFS. IFRS 18 · para 16–17 KPMG FI 2024 · section 4.1 EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.1 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 2.1 Third decision — does the grouping obscure? Aggregate items with shared characteristics, disaggregate items with characteristics that are not shared, and do not obscure material information. IFRS 18 · para 41–42 KPMG FI 2024 · section 4.2 EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.2 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 2.2 Fourth decision — does the label tell users what is inside? Label and describe totals, subtotals, line items and note items so they faithfully represent the item’s characteristics, and use ‘other’ only when no more informative label exists. IFRS 18 · para 43 IFRS 18 · para B25 KPMG FI 2024 · section 4.3 EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.3 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 2.3 Trap: PFS versus notes is not a simple “summary versus dump” pipeline. Materiality determines whether information is required; the defined roles, aggregation principles and faithful labels determine how and where it is provided. IFRS 18 · para 15–20 IFRS 18 · para 41–43"
