    "chapter": "Chapter 2 — PFS roles & aggregation",
    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch02.html#s-2-0-1",
    "text": "2.0.1 — Materiality — definition, obscuring and the four-step process Information is material if omitting, misstating or obscuring it could reasonably be expected to influence decisions that primary users of general purpose financial statements make on the basis of those statements , which provide financial information about a specific reporting entity. IFRS 18 · para B1 Materiality depends on nature or magnitude, alone or in combination, is judged against primary users and their characteristics, and cuts both ways. Nature or magnitude, alone or in combination: materiality depends on the nature or magnitude of information, or both, assessed individually or in combination with other information in the context of the financial statements as a whole. Because the definition already covers individual and collective assessment, paragraph 19 needs no separate “individually or collectively” wording. IFRS 18 · para B2 IFRS 18 · para BCZ51 Primary users and their characteristics: the primary users are existing and potential investors, lenders and other creditors who cannot require reporting entities to provide information directly to them and must rely on general purpose financial statements. They have a reasonable knowledge of business and economic activities and review the information diligently, although even well-informed users may need an adviser for complex economic phenomena. The entity considers those user characteristics together with its own circumstances. IFRS 18 · para B4 IFRS 18 · para B5 Materiality cuts both ways: it is not only about excluding information. The IASB amended IAS 1 to highlight that materiality applies to specifically required disclosures and that it also involves decisions to include additional information — now retained in paragraph 20 of IFRS 18. IFRS 18 · para BCZ50 IFRS 18 · para 20 Obscuring circumstance Example in the Standard Related IFRS 18 control Vague language Material information is disclosed, but the language used is vague or unclear. IFRS 18 · para B3(a) Faithful labels and descriptions — 2.4 . IFRS 18 · para 43 Scattered information Material information about an item, transaction or other event is scattered throughout the financial statements. IFRS 18 · para B3(b) Cross-references and systematic notes — 2.1.4 . IFRS 18 · para 114 Over-aggregation Dissimilar items, transactions or other events are inappropriately aggregated. IFRS 18 · para B3(c) Disaggregate on characteristics that are not shared — 2.3 . IFRS 18 · para 41(b) Over-disaggregation Similar items, transactions or other events are inappropriately disaggregated. IFRS 18 · para B3(d) Aggregate on shared characteristics — 2.3 . IFRS 18 · para 41(a) Material hidden by immaterial Understandability is reduced because material information is hidden by immaterial information to the extent that a primary user cannot determine what information is material. IFRS 18 · para B3(e) Disaggregate a material item when immaterial information obscures it — 2.4 . IFRS 18 · para B24(b) Figure 8 places the PFS and notes roles inside Step 3 of the materiality process in IFRS Practice Statement 2 Making Materiality Judgements . Figure 8 accompanies IFRS 18 and is not part of it. IFRS 18 · Figure 8 Step What Figure 8 shows Step 1 — Identify Inputs: the requirements of IFRS Standards and knowledge about primary users’ common information needs. Step 2 — Assess Inputs: quantitative factors and qualitative factors. Step 3 — Organise Organise the material information identified in Step 2 within the draft financial statements: the entity exercises judgement in communicating information clearly and concisely (Practice Statement 2 paragraph 56) and considers the different roles of the PFS and the notes in deciding whether to present an item separately, aggregate it with other information or disclose it in the notes (Practice Statement 2 paragraph 58). IFRS 18 supplies those roles — useful structured summaries for the PFS and material information for the notes. IFRS 18 · para 16 IFRS 18 · para 17 Step 4 — Review Review the draft financial statements."
