    "chapter": "Chapter 2 — PFS roles & aggregation",
    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch02.html#s-2-1",
    "text": "2.1 Roles — PFS versus notes Use the roles as a location test. The Standard says: To achieve the objective of financial statements … an entity presents information in the primary financial statements and discloses information in the notes. It then defines what each location is for. IFRS 18 · para 15 Location / role Standard extract Location test Practical consequence PFS Structured summaries useful for an understandable overview, comparisons between entities or periods, and identifying areas where users may seek more information. IFRS 18 · para 16 Does the line or subtotal help users see the shape of the entity, compare it, or find a note area? Present useful structured summaries of assets, liabilities, equity, income, expenses and cash flows. KPMG FI 2024 · section 4.1 EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.1 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 2.1 Notes Provide material information necessary to understand PFS line items and to supplement the PFS to achieve the objective of financial statements. IFRS 18 · para 17 Is more detail needed to understand the presented line, or is material information needed beyond the summary? Notes are complementary, not “less important”: they provide additional material information and are more detailed or disaggregated. KPMG FI 2024 · section 4.1 EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.1 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 2.1 Extent The different roles mean PFS information is more aggregated, while notes contain more detailed information, including disaggregation of information presented in the PFS. IFRS 18 · para 18 Do not force every material item onto the face of a PFS; do not treat notes as an unstructured residual. The roles guide where material information is supplied; materiality does not itself decide location. EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.1 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 2.1 Specific requirements still matter, and each PFS line item remains a matter of judgement. Specific requirements still matter. Other Standards may specify presentation or disclosure. An immaterial result need not be provided even where a requirement is described as a minimum, but the entity considers additional disclosures where the specific requirements do not enable user understanding. IFRS 18 · para 19–20 PFS line-item judgement. A material item is not automatically a separate PFS line: the entity considers whether separate presentation contributes to a useful structured summary. If material information is not presented in the PFS, it is disclosed in the notes. IFRS 18 · para 21–24 KPMG FI 2024 · section 4.1 EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.1.1 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 2.1.1 Illustration: 2.1.1 — Present a line item? IFRS 18 · para 15–17 IFRS 18 · para 16 IFRS 18 · para BC55 IFRS 18 · para 114 KPMG FI 2024 · section 4.1 EY Closer look 2026 · Ill 2-1 / section 2.1 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · Ill 2-1 / section 2.1 Facts An entity undertakes a major restructuring during the reporting period and concludes that information about the restructuring is material . Question: should the statement of profit or loss present a separate line item for ‘restructuring expenses’ ? Materiality is already settled; the open question is location — does a separate line contribute to a useful structured summary under the PFS role? Each of the three aspects of the PFS role is tested; any one of them can support separate presentation. Assessment (a) Understandable overview of the entity’s expenses for the period. Separate presentation could help users understand an increase in total operating expenses for the current period. (b) Comparisons between entities or between reporting periods. Separately presenting restructuring expenses, because they lack persistence, could make other line items more comparable to the entity’s prior-period amounts and to amounts presented by other entities, aiding trend analysis. (c) Items about which users may seek more information in the notes. When the notes include additional information about restructuring expenses, separate presentation on the face — with a cross-reference to the related note — could draw users’ attention to that information. Decision Present the line when it is necessary for the statement of profit or loss to provide a useful structured summary. Otherwise disclose in the notes. Material information that is not presented on the face is disclosed in the notes. Material does not mean an automatic face line. Presentation follows the defined PFS role; the notes carry the remaining material detail. Cross-reference. A presented line is cross-referenced to the restructuring note; paragraph 114 also requires a note to identify the line item(s) that include its amounts."
