    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch02.html#s-2-1-2",
    "text": "2.1.2 — Why the roles exist — user attention, prominence and clutter The PFS and notes roles exist because users rely more on the PFS, and the role descriptions serve two purposes. More prominent is not more important, digital access needs the same structure, not all material information fits on the face, and the Standard distinguishes item, line item and other material information. Users rely more on the PFS: feedback and research showed that users pay more attention to information presented in the PFS, and use it more often, because the PFS (a) Give an overview of financial performance, financial position, cash flows and changes in equity; (b) Are more structured than the notes and can be easier to use to compare entities; and (c) Can be used to identify items or areas to seek more information on. The roles were developed from the Conceptual Framework, that feedback and research on how users use financial statements IFRS 18 · para BC45 EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.1 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 2.1 Two purposes for the descriptions: the roles help an entity judge whether to present information in the PFS or disclose it in the notes, and they will help the IASB when it develops new or revised presentation and disclosure requirements. IFRS 18 · para BC46 KPMG FI 2024 · section 4.1 More prominent is not more important: because each PFS provides a useful structured summary, PFS information is more prominent than note information — but greater prominence does not make the notes secondary to, or less important than, the PFS. The notes have a different role in both digital and paper-based reporting. IFRS 18 · para BC49 EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.1 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 2.1 Digital access needs the same structure: a useful structured summary gives the context in which individually tagged items can be understood — for example, operating profit is identified as a subtotal that includes all income and expenses classified in the operating category, and a specific operating expense is identified as included in operating profit. The summary is as important for digitally accessed information as for paper. IFRS 18 · para BC62–BC63 Not all material information fits on the face: the role refers to an understandable overview (the Conceptual Framework’s understandability characteristic). Presenting many income and expense items as line items would clutter the statement and make the overview hard to obtain; the entity’s specific facts and circumstances determine what gives an understandable overview. IFRS 18 · para BC54 KPMG FI 2024 · section 4.1 EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.1.1 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 2.1.1 Item, line item and other material information: an item is an asset, liability, equity instrument or reserve, income, expense or cash flow, or any aggregation or disaggregation of them; a line item is an item presented separately in the PFS; other material information about items is disclosed in the notes. IFRS 18 · para 41 EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.1.1 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 2.1.1"
