    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch02.html#s-2-2-3",
    "text": "2.2.3 — Realised and unrealised fair value gains and losses Can realised and unrealised fair value gains and losses on items measured at fair value through profit or loss be presented as separate line items? In some industries, such as asset management, the split is an important metric, and some regulators and national GAAPs require it — but IFRS Accounting Standards define neither term and give no calculation guidance. Two tests apply. IFRS 18 · para 41(c) IFRS 18 · para B78 IFRS 18 · para 24(a) EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.1.1.B · Q2-1 Test Analysis (a) Disaggregation criteria Whether a gain or loss is realised or unrealised is a characteristic. It is not listed in B78, but it may give material information about measurement or outcome uncertainty, and a single dissimilar characteristic can make disaggregated information material. If separate presentation does not meet the line-item test, separate note disclosure may still be required when the information is material — even with no specific disclosure requirement. IFRS 18 · para B78(e) IFRS 18 · para B22 IFRS 18 · para 41(d) EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.1.1.B · Q2-1 (b) “In accordance with IFRS Accounting Standards” The total fair value gain or loss recognised in profit or loss is recognised and measured in accordance with IFRS Accounting Standards; splitting it is neither explicitly nor implicitly precluded. An accounting policy appropriately developed under IAS 8 is in accordance with IFRS Accounting Standards, which may suggest the split does not conflict with paragraph 24(a). The policy considers requirements for similar and related issues — for example IFRS 13 paragraph 93(f) on unrealised gains or losses for Level 3 fair value measurements. IFRS 18 · para 24(a) EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.1.1.B · Q2-1 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 2.1.1.B · Q2-1 Practice on presenting realised and unrealised fair value gains and losses may differ, and judgement, labelling and policy disclosure apply if the split is presented. Practice may differ: with no specific guidance, different practices may develop; local regulation may require separate presentation in some industries or jurisdictions. EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.1.1.B · Q2-1 If presented: judgement decides whether the split contributes to a useful structured summary; if it does, label the line item accurately and disclose the accounting policy for determining the realised and unrealised parts. IFRS 18 · para 43 EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.1.1.B · Q2-1 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 2.1.1.B · Q2-1"
