    "text": "2.3.2 — Characteristics to consider — statement of profit or loss and statement of financial position The application guidance lists characteristics an entity considers when judging whether items are similar or dissimilar — for income and expenses in B78, and for assets, liabilities and equity in B110. KPMG’s examples (nature, function, measurement basis, size, geographical location, regulatory environment) are drawn from these lists; the examples column follows EY. IFRS 18 · para B19 IFRS 18 · para B78 IFRS 18 · para B110 KPMG FI 2024 · section 4.2 EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.2.2.A EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 2.2.2.A Characteristic Income and expenses Assets, liabilities, equity Examples Nature Yes (with paragraph 80). IFRS 18 · para B78(a) Yes. IFRS 18 · para B110(a) Transactions: depreciation of property, plant and equipment; staff costs. Balances: equity investments; debt investments. Function (role) in business activities Yes (with paragraph 81). IFRS 18 · para B78(b) Yes. IFRS 18 · para B110(b) Selling, distribution and administrative expenses; investment property. Measurement basis Yes. IFRS 18 · para B78(d) Yes. IFRS 18 · para B110(e) Fair value through profit or loss; amortised cost. Measurement or outcome uncertainty (or other risks) Yes. IFRS 18 · para B78(e) Yes. IFRS 18 · para B110(f) Measurement uncertainty: recoverable amount under IAS 36; lifetime expected credit losses under IFRS 9. Outcome uncertainty: provision for a legal dispute; variable consideration. Size Yes. IFRS 18 · para B78(f) Yes. IFRS 18 · para B110(g) Neither IFRS 18 nor its Basis for Conclusions expands on “size”; it is not immediately clear whether it refers only to quantitative materiality. EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.2.2.A Geographical location or regulatory environment Yes. IFRS 18 · para B78(g) Yes. IFRS 18 · para B110(h) Property, plant and equipment in volatile jurisdictions; jurisdictional requirements for employee benefits. Tax effects Yes. IFRS 18 · para B78(h) Yes. IFRS 18 · para B110(j) Different tax rates apply to the items; assets or liabilities have different tax bases. Persistence Yes. IFRS 18 · para B78(c) — How frequently the item of income or expense occurs; whether it is recurring or non-recurring. Initial recognition or change in estimate Yes. IFRS 18 · para B78(i) — Income or expenses arising on initial recognition of a transaction or event, or from a subsequent change in an estimate relating to it. Duration and timing of recovery or settlement — Yes. IFRS 18 · para B110(c) Whether the item is classified as current or non-current; whether its recovery or settlement forms part of the operating cycle. Liquidity — Yes. IFRS 18 · para B110(d) Cash; receivables; long-term fixed deposits. Type — Yes. IFRS 18 · para B110(i) Type of good; type of service; type of customer. Restrictions on use or transferability — Yes. IFRS 18 · para B110(k) Restricted cash; ring-fenced assets. In the statement of financial position, nature or function decides line items, and judgement applies both on the face and in the notes. Statement of financial position — nature or function decides line items: additional line items for assets and liabilities are judged on their nature or function; characteristics B110(c)–(k) — such as duration, liquidity, measurement basis, type and tax effects — help identify that nature or function. This retains the IAS 1 emphasis on nature and function and aligns with the nature/function analysis of operating expenses in the statement of profit or loss. IFRS 18 · para B109 IFRS 18 · para BC315 EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.2.2.A EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 2.2.2.A Judgement, both for the face and for the notes: paragraphs 24 and 41(c) drive additional PFS line items; paragraphs 20 and 41(d) drive note disaggregation — in both cases based on similar and dissimilar characteristics. IFRS 18 · para B78 IFRS 18 · para B109–B110"
