    "text": "2.3.5 — Operating expenses by function — level of aggregation B85 applies the aggregation principles to operating expenses. Expenses for human resources, information technology, legal and accounting share a characteristic — resources consumed in administrative activities — and might be presented as ‘administrative expenses’. Selling expenses arise from resources consumed in selling activities, a dissimilar characteristic, so separate line items for selling and administrative expenses might be necessary for a useful structured summary. IFRS 18 · para B85 IFRS 18 · para 78 One entity’s facts can support separate lines for operating expenses by function, yet a combined line can also be valid. Separate lines on one entity’s facts: an entity presenting by function puts production costs into cost of sales, aggregates human resources, legal and accounting costs as administrative and commissions and marketing as selling, and concludes that a combined ‘selling and administrative expenses’ line is not appropriate in its circumstances — separate lines give a more understandable overview and may help peer comparison; the notes add the nature of the expenses. Full illustration: Ch5 Illustration 5.1.2 . KPMG FI 2024 · Example 4 / section 4.2 A combined line can also be valid: despite the B85 example, selling and administrative expenses need not always be presented separately. They may share other characteristics; aggregating individually material items may be needed to avoid clutter, provided the aggregated material information is disclosed in the notes; and because the useful structured summary takes a user perspective, an entity might conclude users would not find separate lines important for comparisons or the overview. IFRS 18 · para 16 EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.2.3 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 2.2.3"
