    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch02.html#s-2-4",
    "text": "2.4 Labelling and “other” discipline Faithful grouping needs a faithful label. Label and describe totals, subtotals, line items and note items so the description represents the item’s characteristics and gives users the explanations needed to understand how the item was aggregated or disaggregated. IFRS 18 · para 43 IFRS 18 · para B24 Descriptions must be complete, and aggregation plays out differently for material plus material, material plus immaterial, and immaterial plus immaterial. ‘Other’ is permitted but discouraged. Descriptions must be complete: some entities provided information about ‘unusual’ income or expenses without explaining why they were unusual. Faithful representation requires the descriptions and explanations a user needs — in some cases the meaning of the terms used and how items were aggregated or disaggregated; an entity describing items as ‘unusual’ explains its definition of ‘unusual’. IFRS 18 · para BC77–BC78 KPMG FI 2024 · section 4.3 EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.3 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 2.3 Material plus material. An entity may aggregate material items to summarise information, but must disclose information about each material item when required by the aggregation and disaggregation principles. IFRS 18 · para B24(a) Material plus immaterial. Use a label that faithfully represents the material item; disclose disaggregated information about it if the immaterial information obscures the material information. IFRS 18 · para B24(b) Immaterial plus immaterial. A group may complete a list or subtotal, but the entity still applies the “other” guidance if the aggregated amount could make users reasonably question whether material items are included. IFRS 18 · para B24(c) IFRS 18 · para B26(b) ‘Other’ is permitted but discouraged. KPMG and EY both stress that “other” is discouraged: first look for a more informative label; if none exists, describe the aggregation as precisely as possible and provide further information when the amount could reasonably raise a materiality question. Users raised concerns about large ‘other expenses’ amounts with no information about what they include. IFRS 18 · para BC79 KPMG FI 2024 · section 4.3 EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.3.1 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 2.3.1 “Other” decision Required response Disclosure consequence More informative label exists Use the label that describes the material item or the shared / dissimilar characteristics. Faithful description is the first control; do not default to “other”. Where a material item sits with immaterial items, describing only the material item may give an informative label. IFRS 18 · para B25(a)–(b) KPMG FI 2024 · section 4.3 No more informative label — any aggregation Use the most precise possible label, such as “other operating expenses” or “other finance expenses”. Provide disaggregated material information in the notes. IFRS 18 · para B26(a) Only immaterial items, but the amount is questionable Assess whether users could reasonably question whether material items are included. Explain that no material items are included, or explain the nature and amount of the largest item, as applicable. IFRS 18 · para B26(b) Pitfall: “Other” is not a licence to hide dissimilar leftovers. If the label is unavoidable, make it as precise as possible and resolve any reasonable materiality question with further information. IFRS 18 · para 43 IFRS 18 · para B25–B26"
