    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch02.html#s-2-5",
    "text": "2.5 Offsetting Offsetting is prohibited unless required or permitted, aggregation is not netting, and the prohibition rests on the harm offsetting does to users. No offsetting unless required or permitted. Do not offset assets and liabilities or income and expenses unless an IFRS Accounting Standard requires or permits it; report both sides separately unless the offset reflects the substance of the transaction or other event. IFRS 18 · para 44–45 Aggregation is not netting. The aggregation/disaggregation principles concern how items are grouped and separated; they do not create a general permission to net unlike balances or flows. The offsetting requirements are substantially unchanged from IAS 1. EY Closer look 2026 · section 2.2.1 Why offsetting harms users: outside cases where it reflects substance, offsetting in the statement(s) of financial performance or the statement of financial position reduces users’ ability to understand the transactions, events and conditions that occurred and to assess future cash flows. IFRS 18 · para 45 Situation Treatment Valuation allowances Measuring assets net of valuation allowances — for example obsolescence allowances on inventories and allowances for expected credit losses on financial assets — is not offsetting. IFRS 18 · para 45 Revenue measurement Revenue under IFRS 15 is measured at the consideration the entity expects to be entitled to, reflecting trade discounts and volume rebates. IFRS 18 · para B27 Incidental transactions Results of transactions incidental to the main revenue-generating activities are netted when that reflects substance: (a) gains and losses on disposal of non-current assets, measured as consideration less the carrying amount and related selling expenses; (b) expenditure on an IAS 37 provision reimbursed under a contractual arrangement with a third party (for example a supplier’s warranty) may be netted against the reimbursement. IFRS 18 · para B27(a) IFRS 18 · para B27(b) Groups of similar transactions Gains and losses from a group of similar transactions — for example foreign exchange gains and losses, or gains and losses on financial instruments held for trading — included in the same category of the statement(s) of financial performance are presented net; they are disclosed separately in the notes if that gives material information. IFRS 18 · para B28 IFRS 18 · para 47–68"
