    "text": "3.0 Scope — five P&L categories Classify every profit-or-loss amount into one of five categories. IFRS 18 requires an entity to classify income and expenses included in the statement of profit or loss in the operating, investing, financing, income taxes or discontinued operations category. Operating is the residual/default. Investing and financing follow specified asset and liability logic, with overrides when investing in assets or providing financing to customers is a specified main business activity . Income taxes and discontinued operations close the set. Foreign exchange differences, the net monetary position, derivatives and derecognition follow the cross-category rules in 3.7 . , (selective) IFRS 18 · para 47–68 IFRS 18 · para B29–B72 Classifying an amount across the five P&L categories takes three decisions (which band, whether there is a specified MBA, and whether the amount is residual operating), and general and specific requirements apply together. First decision — which band? Place each P&L amount in exactly one of the five categories; do not invent a sixth “non-recurring” band. IFRS 18 · para 47 IFRS 18 · para B29 Second decision — specified MBA? Assess whether investing in assets or providing financing to customers is a main business activity before applying the investing and financing tests. If yes, some amounts that would otherwise sit in investing or financing are classified in operating instead. IFRS 18 · para 49–51 IFRS 18 · para 55–58 IFRS 18 · para 65–66 Third decision — residual operating? Whatever is not classified in investing, financing, income taxes or discontinued operations lands in operating — including volatile or non-recurring items. IFRS 18 · para 52 IFRS 18 · para IN9 IFRS 18 · para B42 General and specific requirements together. Every entity applies the general classification requirements. An entity with a specified main business activity also applies the specific requirements, but only to the income and expenses they cover; the rest of its income and expenses stay under the general requirements. Specific requirements also exist for issuers of insurance contracts and of investment contracts with participation features. EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.2 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.3 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1 Pitfall: Teaching “operating = recurring only.” Operating is residual/default and gives a complete picture of operations for the period — including volatile items not classified elsewhere. Use an MPM note (Ch6) for management views; do not invent a fake operating filter. IFRS 18 · para 52 IFRS 18 · para IN9 IFRS 18 · para B42"
