    "chapter": "Chapter 3 — P&L categories",
    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-2",
    "text": "3.2 Operating — the default category Operating is residual by design. An entity shall classify in the operating category all income and expenses included in the statement of profit or loss that are not classified in investing, financing, income taxes or discontinued operations. IFRS 18 · para 52 Operating is the default category and gives a complete operations picture, as App B confirms, with operations including supporting activities. The category is residual rather than directly defined because a direct definition would be hard to apply consistently, the Big4 align on it, and it links to operating profit. Complete operations picture. The Introduction states that the operating category is the default and provides a complete picture of an entity’s operations for the reporting period ; it includes, but is not limited to, income and expenses from main business activities regardless of whether they are volatile or non-recurring. IFRS 18 · para IN9 App B confirmation. B42 repeats that operating includes all income and expenses not classified in the other categories applying paragraphs 53–68, including such income or expenses that are volatile or non-recurring , and that main-business results land in operating except equity-accounted investments. IFRS 18 · para B42 Operations include supporting activities. In the Board’s view all income and expenses not relating to another category arise from operations, and operations are not limited to main business activities: supporting activities that do not generate revenue directly also give rise to operating income and expenses. Volatile, unusual or non-recurring items are included because faithful representation of the period’s operations needs them — omitting either the depreciation or the impairment of an item of equipment would not depict the decision to buy and deploy it. IFRS 18 · para BC89 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.3 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.2.3 Why residual, not a direct definition. BC91 explains that a direct definition of operating profit would be difficult to apply consistently for all entities; classifying investing and financing first, then treating operating as default, is expected to be simpler and more consistent. IFRS 18 · para BC91 Big4 alignment. KPMG describes operating as a ‘default’ or ‘residual’ category and stresses that volatile, unusual or non-recurring items are not excluded; EY likewise positions operating as residual so that main-business items (and supporting activities) stay inside operating, including volatile items. Equity-accounted results remain in investing even if management views them as main-business. KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.2.1 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.3 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.2.3 Operating profit link. Operating profit or loss comprises all income and expenses classified in the operating category (presentation detail in Ch4). IFRS 18 · para 70 What it is — default / residual Complete operations picture for the period: whatever is not classified elsewhere, including main-business results and volatile items. IFRS 18 · para 52 IFRS 18 · para IN9 IFRS 18 · para B42 Common trap — “recurring only” Filtering “non-recurring” out of operating invents a category IFRS 18 does not define. Use an MPM note (Ch6) for management views — not a fake operating filter. IFRS 18 · para 52 IFRS 18 · para 117 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.3"
