    "text": "3.2.1 — What the operating category typically captures Type of income and expenses Examples From ‘operating assets’ — assets that do not generate a return individually and largely independently Revenue from goods or services; depreciation, impairment and reversals of PPE; amortisation, impairment and reversals of intangibles; gains and losses on disposal of PPE or intangibles; a bargain purchase gain from a business combination that includes assets giving rise to operating income and expenses. IFRS 18 · para B48–B49 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.2.1 From ‘other liabilities’ (not raising finance only), other than interest and the effect of interest-rate changes Consumption of purchased goods or services (e.g. repairs expensed and recorded as a trade payable); current and past service cost of a defined benefit plan; remeasurements of contingent consideration in a business combination. IFRS 18 · para B54–B55 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.2.1 Additional amounts for entities with a specified main business activity Investing in assets: rental income and fair value gains and losses on investment property; dividends on financial assets. Providing financing to customers: interest income on loans to customers; interest expense on borrowings related to that financing (unrelated borrowings follow the accounting policy choice in 3.5.10 ). IFRS 18 · para 58 IFRS 18 · para 65(a)(i) KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.2.1 Always operating, even outside main business activities Income and expenses from issued investment contracts with participation features recognised under IFRS 9, and insurance finance income and expenses under IFRS 17 (see 3.4.7 ). IFRS 18 · para 64 IFRS 18 · para B58 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.2.1 Never operating, even from a main business activity Income and expenses from equity-accounted investments are always investing (see 3.3.2 ). IFRS 18 · para 55(a) IFRS 18 · para B42 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.2.1 Under IAS 1 some entities report an operating profit subtotal and others do not, and because operating activities are undefined its content varies; IFRS 18 requires the subtotal and indirectly defines its content, so some entities may see significant changes to how they report operating results. Practice change — it depends. Under IAS 1 some entities report an operating profit subtotal and others do not, and because ‘operating activities’ is undefined its content varies. IFRS 18 requires the subtotal and indirectly defines its content, so some entities may see significant changes to how they report operating results. IFRS 18 · para BC89 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.2.1"
