    "chapter": "Chapter 3 — P&L categories",
    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-2-3",
    "text": "3.2.3 — Interest on revenue-related assets and other operating items Item Category Why Interest income on an IFRS 15 contract asset with a significant financing component Operating Like a revenue receivable, the contract asset arises from the supply of goods or services and does not generate a largely independent return. EY view: it looks counterintuitive next to the financing classification of contract-liability interest, but here the entity provides financing to the customer rather than raising it, and assets and liabilities follow different rules. IFRS 18 · para B48(b) EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.3 · Q3-13 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.2.3 · Q3-10 Interest income on an IFRIC 12 service concession financial asset Operating Treated like an IFRS 15 receivable — it arises from producing or supplying goods and services classified in operating. IFRS 18 · para B48(b) EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.3 · Q3-14 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.2.3 · Q3-11 Interest previously capitalised into the cost of a qualifying asset (IAS 23) Follows the asset — operating for a factory building EY view: once capitalised, the expense loses its previous nature; the nature of the asset drives classification of depreciation, impairment or disposal gains. The capitalised Type 1 interest is irrelevant. IFRS 18 · para B49 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.3 · Q3-15 Remeasurement of contingent consideration in a business combination Operating Provided the business combination includes assets that give rise to operating income and expenses. EY view: applies to all contingent consideration, including arrangements that meet the definition of a derivative. IFRS 18 · para B49(f) IFRS 18 · para B55(c) EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.3 · Q3-16 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.2.3 · Q3-12 Government grant income (IAS 20 gross or net) Gross: generally operating. Net: follows the item it is netted against Grant income generally does not arise from a stand-alone-return asset, a Type 1 liability, or identified interest on a Type 2 liability — so gross income falls to the default category. Gross and net presentation can therefore land in different categories. IAS 20 · para 26–27, 29 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.3 · Q3-17 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.2.3 · Q3-13 Illustration: 3.2.4 — Government grant income presented gross or net EY Closer look 2026 · Ill 3-4 / section 3.2.3 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · Ill 3-3 / section 3.2.3 IFRS 18 · para B48(a) IFRS 18 · para 52 IFRS 18 · para 60 Facts Entity A receives a government grant to purchase a new rolling mill, which it uses in combination with other assets to produce goods. It recognises deferred income and releases it over the mill’s useful life; its policy presents grant income gross . Entity B receives a government grant to reimburse the interest expense on its loan with Bank X. The grant is received in full when the loan is taken out, recognised as deferred income and released as the loan interest is paid; its policy also presents grant income gross . Assessment Entity A. The income is the release of deferred income, not a return from the mill (an asset used with others). It does not arise from a Type 1 liability and is not interest identified on a Type 2 liability. Entity B. The income is not from a stand-alone-return asset; it is not part of the initial or subsequent amortised-cost accounting for the Bank X loan under IFRS 9, so not Type 1 income; and it is not identified interest on a Type 2 liability. Decision Gross policy — both entities: classify the grant income in operating . Net policy — Entity A: the grant reduces depreciation on the mill, which stays in operating . Net policy — Entity B: the grant reduces interest expense on the Type 1 Bank X loan, which is classified in financing . Gross policy in practice: EY sees gross grant income as generally operating, but practice may develop and should be monitored."
