    "text": "3.3 Investing category Investing separates stand-alone returns from operations. Except as required by paragraphs 55–58 for an entity that has a specified main business activity, classify in investing the paragraph 54 income and expenses from the assets listed in paragraph 53. IFRS 18 · para 53 The investing category captures three asset sets (paragraph 53) and moves specified amounts (paragraph 54), distinguishing stand-alone from combination assets. The band exists so investors can analyse stand-alone investment returns separately from operations, some assets keep their own rules, and the Big4 align on the approach. Three asset sets (paragraph 53). Associates, joint ventures and unconsolidated subsidiaries (B43–B44). Cash and cash equivalents. Other assets that generate a return individually and largely independently of the entity’s other resources (B45–B49). IFRS 18 · para 53 What amounts move (paragraph 54). For those assets, classify in investing: (a) The income generated by the assets; (b) Income and expenses from initial and subsequent measurement, including on derecognition; and (c) Incremental expenses directly attributable to acquiring and disposing of the assets (e.g. transaction costs, costs to sell) IFRS 18 · para 54 IFRS 18 · para B47 Stand-alone vs combination assets. B46 typically lists debt/equity investments and investment properties (and related rent receivables) as stand-alone-return assets. B48 assets used in combination to produce or supply goods or services (e.g. PPE, receivables from goods/services) do not generate a return individually and largely independently — their income and expenses stay in operating. IFRS 18 · para B45–B49 Why the band exists. The investing category enables investors to analyse returns from stand-alone investments separately from an entity’s operations. Investing relates to assets, while financing generally relates to liabilities; many assets generate income as part of operations, so only specified income and expenses from certain assets move to investing. IFRS 18 · para IN10 IFRS 18 · para BC103 IFRS 18 · para BC105 IFRS 18 · para BC135 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.1 Assets with their own rules. Derivatives and designated hedging instruments ( 3.7.8 ) and defined benefit plan assets ( 3.4.2 ) sit outside the paragraph 53(c) residual and can produce financing amounts. EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.1 Big4 alignment. KPMG and EY both summarise the investing category around the same three asset sets and the paragraph 54 income/expense package; EY notes typical items such as rental income and remeasurements of investment property, interest/FV changes on financial assets, dividends, equity-accounted results, and cash & cash equivalents. KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.2.2 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.1 Paragraph 53 asset set Default category (no SMBA pull) Specified-MBA override Associates / JVs / unconsol. subsidiaries Investing for paragraph 54 amounts. If investing in assets is an MBA: equity-method results stay in investing ; non-equity-method results move to operating . IFRS 18 · para 55 IFRS 18 · para 53(a) IFRS 18 · para 55 IFRS 18 · para B38 IFRS 18 · para B43–B44 Cash and cash equivalents Investing for paragraph 54 amounts. Move to operating when paragraph 56(a)/(b) applies (financial-asset MBA, or customer-finance MBA with related cash); accounting-policy choice / all-operating fallback in paragraphs 56(b)–57. No separate “cash MBA” assessment. IFRS 18 · para 56–57 IFRS 18 · para B39 IFRS 18 · para 53(b) IFRS 18 · para 56–57 IFRS 18 · para B39 Other stand-alone-return assets Investing for paragraph 54 amounts. If the entity invests in those assets as an MBA, classify paragraph 54 amounts in operating . IFRS 18 · para 58 IFRS 18 · para B40 IFRS 18 · para 53(c) IFRS 18 · para 58 IFRS 18 · para B45–B49 Concept insight: Investing is not IAS 7 “investing activities.” The labels can look similar, but the P&L investing category is built for stand-alone returns, not cash-flow classification. Proceeds from selling PPE are investing cash flows, yet the gain or loss on that sale is operating, because PPE is used in combination with other resources. IFRS 18 · para BC86–BC87 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.1 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.2.2"
