    "text": "3.3.2 — Associates, joint ventures and unconsolidated subsidiaries Equity-accounted results are always investing. Their placement inside investing, disposal gains with the share of profit, separate financial statements, investees that are not equity-accounted, and the transition option complete the picture. Equity-accounted results are always investing. All entities — whether or not investing in such assets is a main business activity — classify the share of profit or loss and other income and expenses (e.g. impairment) from equity-accounted associates, joint ventures and unconsolidated subsidiaries in investing. The entity does not control those investees, many users analyse them separately, and classifying them in operating would disrupt analyses of both operating margin and the investments; their returns are generated individually and largely independently. IFRS 18 · para 53(a) IFRS 18 · para 55(a) IFRS 18 · para BC110–BC111 IFRS 18 · para BC114–BC115 IFRS 18 · para BC119 IFRS 18 · para BC121 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.3.1 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.1.B EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.2.1.A Placement inside investing. The Standard does not fix where the line sits within investing, so it can be the first line after operating profit, followed by an additional subtotal for operating profit plus equity-accounted results; the line can also be split by investments with similar characteristics, with a subtotal for only some of them. EY view: this is a change for entities that present such results above operating profit today, eased by the additional subtotal where it supports a useful structured summary. IFRS 18 · para BC120 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.3.1 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.1.B EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.2.1.A Disposal gains with the share of profit. EY view: aggregating gains or losses on disposal of an equity-accounted investment with the share of profit can be acceptable if it contributes to a useful structured summary (judge persistence, function, size); separate lines are equally acceptable. IFRS 18’s first illustrative statement shows the aggregated form. IFRS 18 · para IE7 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.1.B · Q3-3 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.2.1.A · Q3-5 Separate financial statements. A parent that equity-accounts subsidiaries in separate financial statements (IAS 27) classifies those results in investing too. IFRS 18 · para BC129 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.1.B EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.2.1.A Not equity-accounted. Associates, joint ventures and unconsolidated subsidiaries measured at fair value or at cost are investing unless the entity invests in them as a main business activity (see 3.5.8 ). IFRS 18 · para 53(a) IFRS 18 · para 55(b) EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.1.B EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.2.1.A Transition option. Entities eligible under IAS 28 paragraph 18 may, on first applying IFRS 18, change their election for an associate or joint venture from the equity method to fair value through profit or loss. IFRS 18 · para C7 IFRS 18 · para BC423 IAS 28 · para 18 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.3.1"
