    "chapter": "Chapter 3 — P&L categories",
    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-4",
    "text": "3.4 Financing category Split liabilities first, then classify the specified income and expenses. To determine what to classify in financing, distinguish liabilities that arise from transactions involving only the raising of finance from other liabilities. IFRS 18 · para 59 The financing category rests on the raising-finance test (B50), which separates raising-finance liabilities (paragraph 60) from other liabilities (paragraph 61), and the Big4 align on it. Raising-finance test (B50). In such transactions the entity (a) receives finance in the form of cash, an extinguishment of a financial liability, or its own equity instruments; and (b) will later return cash or its own equity instruments. Examples include cash-settled debt instruments (loans, bonds), certain supplier-finance arrangements after the payable is derecognised, bonds settled in own shares, and obligations to purchase own equity instruments. IFRS 18 · para B50–B51 Raising-finance liabilities (paragraph 60). Except as set out in paragraphs 63–66, classify in financing: (a) income and expenses from initial and subsequent measurement, including on derecognition; and (b) incremental expenses directly attributable to issue and extinguishment (e.g. transaction costs). IFRS 18 · para 60 IFRS 18 · para B52 Other liabilities (paragraph 61). Except as set out in paragraphs 63–64, classify in financing only (a) interest income and expenses and (b) income and expenses arising from changes in interest rates — and only if the entity identifies those amounts for applying other IFRS requirements. Other changes on those liabilities (e.g. remeasurement of a provision beyond the interest/rate effects) stay outside financing. IFRS 18 · para 61 IFRS 18 · para B53–B55 Big4 alignment. KPMG and EY both stress the Type-1 (raising-finance only) versus Type-2 (other) liability split: Full measurement/derecognition/issue costs for Type 1 Interest and interest-rate-change amounts only for Type 2 (e.g. lease liabilities, pension net interest, unwinding of discounts on provisions). ‘Type 1’ and ‘Type 2’ are EY’s labels KPMG says ‘financing liabilities’ and ‘other liabilities’ KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.2.3 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.2 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.2.2 Liability type What goes to financing What stays out Raising-finance only (e.g. bank loans, bonds) Measurement / derecognition income & expenses + incremental issue/extinguishment costs — subject to paragraphs 63–66. Derivatives / designated hedges follow B70–B76, not the paragraph 60 path. IFRS 18 · para 59(a) IFRS 18 · para 60 IFRS 18 · para 63 IFRS 18 · para B50–B52 Other liabilities (e.g. leases, pensions, trade payables with identified interest) Interest income/expense and interest-rate-change income/expense only if identified for applying other IFRS requirements. Other changes (e.g. service cost, provision remeasurement beyond interest/rate effects) — operating residual. IFRS 18 · para 59(b) IFRS 18 · para 61 IFRS 18 · para B53–B55 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.1.1 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.1.1 Hybrid contracts follow B56–B57, always-operating exclusions keep certain amounts out of financing, and a customer-finance MBA pulls related raising-finance amounts into operating. Hybrid contracts. B56–B57 set out how paragraphs 59–61 apply to hybrid contracts that contain a host that is a liability — see 3.4.5 . IFRS 18 · para 62 IFRS 18 · para B56–B57 Always-operating exclusions from financing. Exclude from financing and classify in operating: (a) income and expenses from issued investment contracts with participation features recognised applying IFRS 9; and (b) insurance finance income and expenses included in P&L applying IFRS 17. IFRS 18 · para 64 IFRS 18 · para B58 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.2.1 Customer-finance MBA. If providing financing to customers is a main business activity, paragraphs 65–66 pull some raising-finance amounts into operating (related liabilities) and keep paragraph 61 amounts in financing — see 3.5.11 . IFRS 18 · para 65–66 IFRS 18 · para B59 Concept insight: The financing category and the subtotal for profit before financing and income taxes enable investors to analyse performance before the effects of financing. IFRS 18 · para IN11 IFRS 18 · para 71"
