    "chapter": "Chapter 3 — P&L categories",
    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-4-1",
    "text": "3.4.1 — Type 1 liabilities — raising finance only Transaction Only raising finance? Why Debt instrument settled in cash — debentures, loans, notes, corporate bonds, mortgages Yes Receives cash; returns cash later. Supplier finance arrangement liability after the payable is derecognised Yes Discharged of a financial liability; returns cash. Bond settled by delivering (a variable number of) own shares Yes Receives cash; returns own equity instruments. Obligation to purchase own equity instruments Yes Receives own equity instruments; returns cash. Payables for goods and services; lease liabilities; defined benefit liabilities; decommissioning, restoration and litigation provisions No Finance is not received in the B50 form. Contract liabilities No Settled by providing goods or services, not cash or own equity. All measurement amounts on Type 1 liabilities go to financing, issue and extinguishment costs do too, and a liability acquired in a business combination raises a further question. All measurement amounts go to financing. Examples — interest expense on issued debt, fair value gains and losses on a liability designated at FVTPL, dividends on shares classified as liabilities, and remeasurements on extinguishing a debt instrument: Interest expense on issued debt Fair value gains and losses on a liability designated at FVTPL Dividends on shares classified as liabilities Remeasurements on extinguishing a debt instrument IFRS 18 · para B52 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.2.A IFRS 18 · para B50–B51 IFRS 18 · para B53 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.2.3 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.2.2.A Issue and extinguishment costs too. Without paragraph 60(b), costs on an amortised-cost liability would reach financing through the effective interest rate while the same costs on an FVTPL liability would be expensed in operating. The costs are typically external and readily identifiable, mirroring the investing rule for assets. IFRS 18 · para 60(b) IFRS 18 · para BC161–BC163 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.2.A EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.2.2.A Acquired in a business combination. EY view: a liability the acquiree properly classified as Type 1 keeps that nature, and so its classification, in the acquirer’s consolidated statements. IFRS 18 · para B50 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.2.A · Q3-10"
