    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-4-7",
    "text": "3.4.7 — Finance costs line and the paragraph 64 exclusions IFRS 18 requires no ‘finance costs’ line and distinguishes insurance finance income and expenses, investment contracts with participation features (IFRS 9), and investment contracts without participation features. No required ‘finance costs’ line. IFRS 18 removes the IAS 1 requirement to present finance costs. The entity judges which lines to present in financing; if financing expenses are presented as one line (e.g. interest on borrowings and lease liabilities), material disaggregation goes in the notes. Items often included in IAS 1 finance costs — FX, derivative gains and losses, derecognition gains and losses — have their own rules and may not be financing ( 3.7 ). IFRS 18 · para 59–66 IFRS 18 · para 75 IFRS 18 · para BC242–BC243 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.2.3 Insurance finance income and expenses. Always operating, for issued insurance contracts, reinsurance held and investment contracts with participation features within IFRS 17 — whether or not issuing insurance is a main business activity. A non-insurer that issues an insurance contract also classifies them in operating; separate guidance for such entities would add complexity. IFRS 18 · para 64(b) IFRS 18 · para BC196–BC197 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.2.3 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.5.B EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.3.5.B Investment contracts with participation features (IFRS 9). Operating for all issuers — e.g. contracts issued by an insurer that are not IFRS 17 contracts with discretionary participation features, or issued by an investment entity. Otherwise the asset returns would sit in operating and the liability expense in financing, splitting a margin that is typically an important performance indicator. IFRS 18 · para 64(a) IFRS 18 · para B58 IFRS 18 · para BC192–BC195 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.6 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.3.6 Investment contracts without participation features. Not excluded — the liability follows the general Type 1 / Type 2 rules. IFRS 18 · para BC194 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.6 · Q3-41"
