    "chapter": "Chapter 3 — P&L categories",
    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-5-3",
    "text": "3.5.3 — Unit of assessment, timing and disclosure A specified MBA is assessed for individual assets or groups, with no assessment for cash or equity-accounted investments. The assessment is made at a point in time and applied prospectively, and the disclosure serves a stated purpose. Individual assets or groups. Assess an individual asset or a group with shared characteristics. Groups of financial assets follow the IFRS 7 classes; groups of non-equity-accounted investments in separate financial statements follow the IAS 27 paragraph 10 measurement categories (e.g. associates under IFRS 9 and subsidiaries at cost are assessed separately). IFRS 18 · para B38 IFRS 18 · para B40 IFRS 18 · para BC147 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.1.2 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.1.A No assessment for cash or equity-accounted investments. Cash classification depends on the financial-asset and customer-financing activities, not on investing in cash; equity-accounted results are investing either way. IFRS 18 · para B38–B39 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.1.2 Point in time, applied prospectively. The assessment uses the facts at the time, not only at the reporting date; a changed outcome applies from the date of change and comparatives are not reclassified. KPMG example: after acquiring a real estate business on 1 July 20X7, a calendar-year entity does not move earlier or comparative investment property amounts from investing to operating — it gives the paragraph 51(c) disclosures. This differs from the retrospective approach to other classification changes in paragraphs 30–40. IFRS 18 · para B41 IFRS 18 · para 51(c) IFRS 18 · para BC101–BC102 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.1.3 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.1.A Disclosure purpose. Disclosing the fact of a specified MBA helps users understand the structure of the statement, especially in digital reports; it is separate from describing the nature of operations under paragraph 116(b). The change disclosures ease the disruption to trend analysis. IFRS 18 · para 51 IFRS 18 · para BC100 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.1.C EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.3.1.A Illustration: 3.5.4 — Entity X — specified MBAs and operating segments KPMG FI 2024 · Example 1 / section 2.1.1 IFRS 18 · para B30 IFRS 18 · para B36 IFRS 18 · para BC97 Facts Entity X applies IFRS 8. It has four business activities: Activities 1 and 2 (not specified MBAs), Activity 3 (providing financing to customers) and Activity 4 (investing in assets), across Operating Segments A, B and C and Reportable Segments A and B. Operating Segment B comprises solely Activity 3 but is not the only business activity in Reportable Segment A. Reportable Segment B comprises solely Activity 4. Internally and externally, X uses a subtotal similar to gross profit that includes the result of Operating Segment B. No amounts are given. Assessment Activities 1 and 2: business activities, but not specified ones — no assessment needed. Activity 3: a single-activity operating segment inside a wider reportable segment, so X must judge whether its performance is an important indicator; the gross-profit-like subtotal shows it is. Activity 4: a reportable segment comprising a single business activity. Decision Providing financing to customers is a specified main business activity. Investing in assets is a specified main business activity. Segments are evidence, not a mechanical segment-to-MBA mapping."
