    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-5-5",
    "text": "3.5.5 — Group, subsidiary and separate financial statements Each reporting entity assesses its MBA for itself, which plays out differently for a subsidiary-only MBA, a holding company with no other substantive activities (IFRS IC, March 2026), and a holding company with its own operations. Each reporting entity assesses for itself. Consolidated, separate and individual statements each report their own specified MBAs, even if consolidation adjustments are then needed; a holding-company parent can differ from both its group and its subsidiaries. IFRS 18 · para B37 IFRS 18 · para BC98–BC99 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.1.A EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.3.1 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.1.1 Subsidiary-only MBA. A financial conglomerate’s insurance subsidiary identifies investing in assets and its banking subsidiary identifies providing financing to customers, but the group identifies only investing in assets because the bank is small relative to the group. The customer-financing rules for Type 1 liabilities then do not apply in the consolidated statements. EY view: follow the group-level assessment; subsidiaries may need to track classification separately for group reporting. IFRS 18 · para B37 IFRS 18 · para BC98 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.1.A · Q3-22 Holding company with no other substantive activities (IFRS IC, March 2026). A parent holding subsidiaries at cost under IAS 27, managing acquisitions and disposals and distributing returns, with no gross-profit-like subtotal and no segment analysis: having no main business activity is inconsistent with the premise that operations land in operating; the absence of the B34/B36 indicators and of the B31 examples is not determinative. Investing in unconsolidated subsidiaries is a main business activity, so cost-method income and expenses are operating. IFRS 18 · para 55(b) IFRS 18 · para B31 IFRS 18 · para B34 IFRS 18 · para B36 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.1.B · Q3-24 Holding company with its own operations. Consider other evidence, such as whether it was established to shape the group by acquiring, managing and selling subsidiaries, or mainly to run its own activities. EY view: practice is mixed today and regulators may have views. EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.1.B · Q3-24 Illustration: 3.5.6 — Parent P and Parent Q — different conclusions within a group KPMG FI 2024 · Example 2 / section 2.1.1.1 IFRS 18 · para B37 IFRS 18 · para BC98–BC99 Facts Scenario A. Parent P is a car manufacturer; all subsidiaries are in car manufacturing or related activities except Subsidiary S, an investment property company. S reports a single reportable segment of investing in property. In the consolidated statements investing in property is not an operating segment on its own, and the group uses no gross-profit-like subtotal that includes it. Scenario B. Parent Q is a holding company with diverse subsidiaries, including R, a retail bank. Q’s separate statements report one segment — investing in subsidiaries; R reports one segment — providing financing to customers; R’s activity is a separate reportable segment of the group. Assessment A — S: its reportable segment comprises a single activity of investing in property. A — group: the activity is not an important indicator of group performance. B — R and group: each has a reportable segment comprising solely customer financing. B — Q: it does not provide financing, so that assessment is irrelevant; it invests in subsidiaries. Decision A: S has an investing-in-assets MBA; the group does not. Consolidation adjustment: S’s rental income moves from operating (S’s statements) to investing (group statements), with knock-on effects in the statement of cash flows. B: R and the group have a customer-financing MBA; Q has only an investing-in-assets MBA (its subsidiaries)."
