    "text": "3.5.9 — Investing in assets as a main business activity — what changes Investing in assets as an MBA changes classification so that investment returns such as rental income and remeasurements can faithfully represent operations, moves certain amounts to operating, and reaches unconsolidated subsidiaries. Only the assets invested in as an MBA move, and funding liabilities follow the general rules. Why. For an investment property company, rental income and remeasurements are the operating result; leaving them in investing would not faithfully represent its operations. Investment returns are an important indicator for such entities. IFRS 18 · para BC94(a) IFRS 18 · para BC104 IFRS 18 · para BC145 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.2 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.3.2 What moves to operating. Paragraph 54 amounts from (a) Associates, JVs and unconsolidated subsidiaries at fair value or cost (paragraph 55 (b)); (b) Cash and cash equivalents when the MBA is investing in financial assets (paragraph 56 (a)); (c) Other stand-alone-return assets such as investment property (paragraph 58). Typical amounts: rent, interest, dividends, depreciation, impairment and reversals, fair value changes, derecognition and held-for-sale amounts IFRS 18 · para 55(b) IFRS 18 · para 56(a) IFRS 18 · para 58 IFRS 18 · para B47 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.2 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.3.2 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.3.1 Unconsolidated subsidiaries. Subsidiaries equity-accounted in separate statements, held by an investment entity at FVTPL, accounted for under IFRS 9, or at cost in separate statements. IFRS 18 · para B44 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.2.A EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.3.2.A Only the assets invested in as an MBA. An investment property company’s other investments (e.g. financial assets not invested in as an MBA) stay investing. IFRS 18 · para BC146 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.2.C EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.3.2.B Funding liabilities follow the general rules. A liability taken on to fund the investments (unless the cost is capitalised) is classified under 3.4 ; the investing MBA does not let financing of that activity sit in operating — unless the entity also has a customer-financing MBA. EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.2 EY Closer look 2026 · section C.3 · Q C.3-3 Amount (entity has the relevant investing MBA) Category Operating-lease rental income from PPE held for leasing Operating EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.2.C · Q3-25 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.3.2.B · Q3-18 Finance income on a net investment in a finance lease — lessor with a customer-financing MBA via finance leases Operating (not largely independent in that business model) IFRS 18 · para B32 IFRS 18 · para BC143 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.2.C · Q3-26 Selling profit or loss on a finance lease of an investment property — entity investing in investment property as an MBA Operating (follows the pre-derecognition category) IFRS 18 · para B60 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.2.C · Q3-27 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.3.2.B · Q3-20 Transaction costs on an equity investment — entity investing in equity instruments as an MBA Operating EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.2.C · Q3-28 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.3.2.B · Q3-21 Maintenance, rates and taxes on investment property Operating (not paragraph 54 amounts in any case) IFRS 18 · para 54 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.2.C · Q3-29 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.3.2.B · Q3-22"
