    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-5-fig33",
    "text": "3.5.16 — Cash and cash equivalents with specified MBA Decision schematic (Official Figure 3.3). Most entities need not ask whether they invest in cash as an MBA: the answer depends on the financial-asset and customer-financing activities. Decision Outcome Does the entity invest in financial assets (scope of paragraph 53(c)) as a main business activity? Yes → classify paragraph 54 income/expenses from cash and cash equivalents in operating (para 56(a)). No → next test. Does the entity provide financing to customers as a main business activity? No → classify in the investing category (para 56). Yes → ask whether the cash/equivalents income or expense relates to providing financing to customers. Relates to providing financing to customers? Yes → classify in operating (para 56(b)(i)). No → accounting policy choice to classify in operating or investing (para 56(b)(ii)); consistent with Fig 3.2 policy where applicable. If cannot distinguish which cash/equivalents relate to customer financing → apply policy to classify all in operating (para 57). Cash treatment differs for financial-asset investors, customer financiers, and entities with both activities. Financial-asset investors. Banks, asset managers and insurers that invest in financial assets (other than cash, associates, JVs and unconsolidated subsidiaries) as an MBA classify all cash income and expenses in operating. EY view: in a group with that MBA this covers cash held anywhere in the group, including cash received as a customer advance. IFRS 18 · para 56(a) IFRS 18 · para BC137–BC138 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.4 · Q3-35 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.3.4 · Q3-24 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.4 · Q3-37 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.3.4 · Q3-26 IFRS 18 · Figure 3.3 IFRS 18 · para 56–57 Customer financiers. Cash related to customer financing (e.g. held for regulatory purposes) → operating; unrelated cash → operating or investing, consistent with the liability policy; indistinguishable → all operating. IFRS 18 · para 56(b) IFRS 18 · para 57 IFRS 18 · para BC139–BC141 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.4 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.3.4 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.3.2 Both activities. KPMG: an entity that also invests in financial assets (e.g. a retail and investment bank) has no cash choice — all cash is operating. EY: because paragraph 56(a) is a requirement rather than a 56(b) choice, there is no cash policy to be consistent with, so the Type 1 liability policy can be elected independently. IFRS 18 · para 56 IFRS 18 · para BC138 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.3.2 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.3.4 · Q3-36 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.3.4 · Q3-25"
