    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-5-banks",
    "text": "3.5.17 — Banks — statement structure A bank’s statement structure depends on which MBAs it has, how it treats non-financial assets, and which subtotals it presents. Which MBAs. A retail and investment bank typically has both; a retail bank typically provides financing to customers and may also invest in financial assets. Every bank’s treasury holds debt and equity investments, so each must assess whether it invests in them as an MBA; certain investment banks may not even have a customer-financing MBA. IFRS 18 · para B32 KPMG FI 2024 · section 8.1.1 EY Closer look 2026 · section C.3 Non-financial assets. Commodities or carbon credits are assessed asset by asset or by group, so a bank’s conclusion may differ by asset type; a bank with a large rental portfolio could also have an investment-property MBA. IFRS 18 · para 58 KPMG FI 2024 · section 8.1.1.6 EY Closer look 2026 · section C.3 · Q C.3-1 Subtotals. Required — operating profit, profit before financing and income taxes (unless the all-operating policy removes it — KPMG 8.1.1.3), and profit or loss: Operating profit Profit before financing and income taxes (unless the all-operating policy removes it — KPMG 8.1.1.3) Profit or loss Common additional subtotals: net interest income, net fee and commission income, total income. IFRS 18 · para 69 IFRS 18 · para 73 KPMG FI 2024 · section 8.1.1.7 EY Figure C.2-1 — retail and investment bank with both MBAs (based on IFRS 18 illustrative example II-4; EY prints no amounts). The bank classifies all unrelated Type 1 liabilities in operating, so it presents no profit-before-financing subtotal. EY Closer look 2026 · Figure C.2-1 / section C.2 Line item Category Why Interest revenue calculated using the effective interest method; interest income on financial assets at FVTPL Operating Loans to customers (customer-financing MBA) and all cash (financial-asset MBA). The FVTPL interest line is shown separately because it is needed for a useful structured summary. Interest expense calculated using the effective interest method; interest expense on financial liabilities at FVTPL Operating Related Type 1 liabilities; the bank also elects operating for unrelated Type 1 liabilities; a central-treasury liability it cannot attribute is operating under paragraph 66. Net interest income ; fee and commission income and expense; net fee and commission income Operating Additional line items and subtotals for a useful structured summary. Credit impairment losses Operating Relate to loans to customers. Net trading income; net gains/(losses) on financial assets and liabilities at FVTPL; net gains/(losses) on derecognition of amortised-cost financial assets Operating Financial-asset MBA (paragraph 58) and the Type 1 operating policy. Other individually immaterial operating income / expenses; impairment of goodwill; employee benefits; depreciation and amortisation Operating Labels convey that the items are individually immaterial; expenses by nature, as most banks present. Operating profit — Required subtotal. Share of profit or loss of, and gains on disposal of, equity-accounted associates and JVs Investing Always investing. Interest expense on litigation provisions; interest expense on pension and lease liabilities Financing Type 2 interest — financing for every entity. Profit before income tax ; income tax expense; profit from continuing operations ; loss from discontinued operations; profit for the year Income taxes / discontinued operations Not re-tested into the other categories."
