    "chapter": "Chapter 3 — P&L categories",
    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-7-5",
    "text": "3.7.5 — Foreign exchange differences and the net monetary position Item giving rise to the amount Category Trade receivable denominated in a foreign currency Operating , with the receivable’s income and expenses. IFRS 18 · para B66(a) Investment in bonds denominated in a foreign currency Investing , unless the entity invests in these assets as a main business activity. KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.6 Debt instrument liability (Type 1) denominated in a foreign currency Financing , unless the entity provides financing to customers as a main business activity and classifies the liability’s income and expenses in operating under paragraph 65. IFRS 18 · para B66(b) IFRS 18 · para 65 Type 2 liability whose income and expenses fall into two categories (e.g. services bought in a foreign currency on extended credit) Judgement: financing or the other category — the whole difference goes to one category; no allocation; similar liabilities treated alike. IFRS 18 · para B67 IFRS 18 · para BC217–BC219 Any item where tracing would involve undue cost or effort Operating , for the affected differences only. IFRS 18 · para B68 IFRS 18 · para BC212 FX related to IAS 12 tax amounts Income taxes . IFRS 18 · para 67 Gain or loss on the net monetary position (hyperinflation) With the associated interest and FX items if presented with them under IAS 29; otherwise operating . IFRS 18 · para B69 IAS 29 · para 28 FX follows the item to give a complete picture of main-business performance, and the undue cost or effort assessment sets a hurdle whose height matters. There is no free choice, the result is imperfect but complete, Type 2 liabilities call for judgement, and the net monetary position has its own rule. Why follow the item. Classifying FX with the related income and expenses gives a complete picture of performance of the main business activities. The Board accepted that many systems capture FX as a single aggregate amount, so it added a per-item undue cost or effort relief instead of a blanket operating rule. IFRS 18 · para BC209–BC212 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.7 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.2.7 IFRS 18 · para B65–B69 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.6 Assessing undue cost or effort. The assessment is made for each item that gives rise to FX differences and is specific to its facts; the same assessment can be applied to items with the same facts. EY adds that it is made transaction by transaction as new items arise and, at each reporting date, for the year’s IAS 21 remeasurements of monetary items. IFRS 18 · para B68 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.7 How high is the hurdle? IFRS 18 does not define undue cost or effort; EY notes the term (also used in IFRS 9) is generally understood not to be a low hurdle. KPMG: it is a lower hurdle than IAS 8 impracticability (“every reasonable effort”), yet not a ‘free pass’ to put FX in operating — though no excessive effort is needed to make the assessment. IAS 8 · para 5 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.6 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.7 No free choice. FX differences go in the category of the related item; they are financing only in specific circumstances. KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.6 Imperfect but complete. The Board acknowledged that operating classification under the relief may leave other categories incomplete, but it keeps operating profit complete. EY calls the relief a pragmatic solution that could involve significant judgement. IFRS 18 · para BC213–BC215 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.7 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.2.7 Type 2 liabilities — the judgement. Ask whether the differences arise from financing decisions (financing), purchasing decisions (operating), or both. A lease liability relates to the right-of-use asset and to financing; a three-month trade payable carries a financing component even when no interest is accounted for. Beyond treating similar liabilities alike there is no further guidance, so EY expects differing conclusions between entities. IFRS 18 · para B67 IFRS 18 · para BC218 IFRS 18 · para 59(b) IFRS 18 · para BC159(b) EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.7 · Q3-20 Net monetary position. If not presented with the associated income and expenses, the gain or loss is operating because in total it will not meet the investing or financing requirements. IFRS 18 · para B69 IFRS 18 · para BC220–BC221 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.7.A EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.2.7.A Illustration: 3.7.6 — FX differences on a lease liability and an interest-free payable EY Closer look 2026 · Ill 3-7 / section 3.2.7 IFRS 18 · para B67 IFRS 18 · para BC218–BC219 Facts An entity recognises IAS 21 foreign exchange differences on two Type 2 liabilities: (a) A lease liability under IFRS 16; (b) A short-term payable for inventory under IFRS 9, with no interest expense recognised because discounting is immaterial. Assessment (a) IFRS 16 treats leases as a form of financing, and the choice between buying and leasing is a financing decision. (b) Buying inventory in a foreign currency is an operating decision, and the payable produces no financing income or expense — putting only its FX in financing could conflict with the follow-the-item principle. Decision (a) Lease liability FX → financing (the entity “might conclude”). (b) Inventory payable FX → operating (the entity “might conclude”). Whichever category is chosen applies to the whole difference and to similar liabilities."
