    "chapter": "Chapter 3 — P&L categories",
    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch03.html#s-3-7-7",
    "text": "3.7.7 — FX on intercompany balances eliminated on consolidation FX on intercompany balances eliminated on consolidation poses a problem on which the Standard gives no guidance, leaving two reasonable readings. The problem. Intercompany balances that are not part of a net investment in a foreign operation are eliminated under IFRS 10, but the related FX differences remain in consolidated profit or loss under IAS 21. At group level there is no related income and expense for the FX to follow. IAS 21 · para 45 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.7 · Q3-21 No guidance in the Standard. KPMG (2024) treats this as a judgemental area. KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.1.6 Two reasonable readings. The IFRS IC discussed the question in March 2026 (as reported by EY) and identified two reasonable readings of B65; the entity sets an IAS 8 accounting policy for its reading and applies it consistently. IFRS 18 · para B65 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.7 · Q3-21 Reading Category Basis (1) Default Operating The related income and expenses are not in consolidated profit or loss, so there is no category to follow. IFRS 18 · para 52 IFRS 18 · para BC89 (2) Pre-elimination category The category the related income and expenses would have had before elimination — or operating if that involves undue cost or effort B65 does not require the related amounts to survive consolidation; assessed from the group’s perspective, so it can differ from the subsidiary’s own classification (e.g. different main business activities). IFRS 18 · para B65 IFRS 18 · para B68 A worked example shows how the two readings play out. Example. A parent lends to a subsidiary with a different functional currency; the loan is in the parent’s currency and is not part of the net investment. The subsidiary treats it as a Type 1 liability and recognises the FX in financing in its own statements; the FX survives into consolidated profit or loss. Group classification: operating under reading (1) or financing under reading (2). EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.2.7 · Q3-21"
