    "text": "3.8 Standard IE Part II — entity P&L shapes Part II gives four statement-of-profit-or-loss shapes so learners can see how category classification and main-business-activity overrides change the face. Amounts are the Official IE figures. IFRS 18 · para IE9–IE13 Illustration: 3.8.1 — AA Group — manufacturer, nature operating expenses IFRS 18 · para IE10 IFRS 18 · para 78 IFRS 18 · para B80–B85 Facts AA Group — manufacturer without a specified main business activity. Presents operating expenses by nature as the most useful structured summary. Official IE10 face amounts for the year ended 31 December 20X2 (comparative 20X1). Assessment Without specified MBA, investing and financing amounts follow the residual operating default after the ordered tests (para 47–68). All operating expenses classified by nature on the face (para 78 / B80–B85). Required subtotals — operating profit, profit before financing and income taxes, and profit or loss (para 69): Operating profit Profit before financing and income taxes Profit or loss (para 69) Baseline manufacturer shape before MBA overrides (contrast BB / CC / DD). Line 20X2 20X1 Revenue 398,700 370,900 Changes in inventories of finished goods and WIP 3,000 (3,700) Raw materials used (146,000) (143,200) Employee benefits (107,000) (104,600) Depreciation, amortisation and impairment (37,500) (36,300) Other operating expenses (17,100) (15,200) Operating profit 94,100 67,900 Share of profit of associates and joint ventures 3,800 2,900 Profit before financing and income taxes 97,900 70,800 Interest expenses on borrowings and lease liabilities (3,500) (4,000) Interest expenses on pension liabilities (6,500) (6,800) Profit before income taxes 87,900 60,000 Income tax expense (21,800) (15,000) PROFIT 66,100 45,000 Illustration: 3.8.2 — BB Group — manufacturer + financing MBA IFRS 18 · para IE11 IFRS 18 · para 49–51 IFRS 18 · para 65–66 Facts BB Group — manufacturer that also provides financing to customers as a specified MBA. Official IE11 face amounts; companion to the financing-MBA classification path. Interest related to customer financing sits in operating; other raising-finance items follow BB’s para 65 policy. Assessment MBA override pulls related Type 1 interest into operating (para 65–66). Still presents para 69 subtotals unless a para 73 path applies. Compare to Entity R ( Illustration 3.5.13 ) for Type 1 / Type 2 detail; use this face for numeric line items. Line 20X2 20X1 Revenue 390,000 365,000 Cost of sales (285,000) (270,000) Gross profit from the sale of goods 105,000 95,000 Interest revenue related to providing financing to customers 119,500 121,000 Interest expenses related to providing financing to customers (110,000) (100,800) Net interest income 9,500 20,200 Selling / R&D / G&A / other operating expenses (aggregated for teaching) (72,100) (70,700) Operating profit 42,400 44,500 Income from investments 5,500 4,000 Profit before financing and income taxes 47,900 48,500 Interest on borrowings not related to customer financing (3,800) (3,500) Interest on pension liabilities (3,600) (4,200) Profit before income taxes 40,500 40,800 Income tax expense (10,125) (10,200) PROFIT 30,375 30,600 Illustration: 3.8.3 — CC Group — insurer investing MBA IFRS 18 · para IE12 IFRS 18 · para 55–58 Facts CC Group — insurer with investing as a specified main business activity. Official IE12 face amounts for the year ended 31 December 20X2. Investing MBA pulls specified investing returns into the operating category. Assessment Returns from assets that generate individual and largely independent returns can sit in operating when investing is a specified MBA (para 55–58). Operating then captures insurance service result plus those investing returns that the MBA overrides. CC is the insurer companion to AA/BB — same ordered tests, different MBA override. Line 20X2 20X1 Insurance revenue 138,200 133,800 Insurance service expenses (107,000) (106,000) Insurance service result 31,200 27,800 Investment income 117,000 103,000 Credit impairment losses (5,000) (1,500) Insurance finance expenses (85,900) (84,000) Net financial result 26,100 17,500 Other operating expenses (3,100) (4,600) Operating profit 54,200 40,700 Share of profit or loss of associates and joint ventures (5,400) 4,800 Profit before financing and income taxes 48,800 45,500 Interest expenses on borrowings and pension liabilities (2,500) (2,200) Profit before income taxes 46,300 43,300 Income tax expense (10,200) (9,000) PROFIT 36,100 34,300 Illustration: 3.8.4 — DD Group — bank (para 73 — no profit-before-financing subtotal) IFRS 18 · para IE13 IFRS 18 · para 65(a)(ii) IFRS 18 · para 73 Facts DD Group — investment and retail bank with investing MBA and financing-to-customers MBA (IE13). Policy (para 65(a)(ii)): classify income/expenses from all Type 1 raising-finance liabilities in operating — including amounts unrelated to customer financing. Presents operating expenses by nature. Assessment Because that policy pulls unrelated raising-finance amounts into operating, DD is not permitted to present profit before financing and income taxes (para 69(b) / para 73). Special-entity path — see Illustration 4.2.1 for the para 73 bridge. DD shows how MBA + para 65 policy changes both category placement and the required-subtotal set. Line 20X2 20X1 Interest revenue 356,000 333,800 Interest expenses (281,000) (259,000) Net interest income 75,000 74,800 Net fee and commission income 31,500 29,500 Net trading income 9,100 900 Net investment income 11,600 7,800 Credit impairment losses (17,300) (19,100) Employee benefits (55,100) (49,500) Depreciation and amortisation (6,700) (5,950) Other operating expenses (5,100) (4,550) Operating profit 43,000 33,900 Share of profit of associates and joint ventures 1,800 2,100 Interest expenses on pension and lease liabilities (2,200) (1,800) Profit before income taxes 42,600 34,200 Income tax expense (11,200) (9,000) PROFIT 31,400 25,200"
