    "chapter": "Chapter 4 — Totals & subtotals",
    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch04.html#s-4-1-2",
    "text": "4.1.2 — Alternatives the Board rejected Alternative considered Board’s response Define operating profit directly (by what it contains) Rejected: a direct definition suitable for all entities would be difficult, if not impossible; it would need more judgement and cost; and it could leave some operating expenses out (e.g. an expense not allocated to a function line). Operating stays the default category ( Ch3 3.2.2 ). IFRS 18 · para BC91(a)–(c) Narrow ‘core’ or ‘normalised’ earnings Rejected: views on operating profit vary, and a narrow definition applied consistently would be hard to develop. Users wanted a consistent, comparable starting point; other tools (additional subtotals, disaggregation, MPMs) carry management’s view. IFRS 18 · para BC92–BC93 Merge investing and financing; drop the middle subtotal Suggested because it would remove classification issues (e.g. cash and cash equivalents), investing may hold few items, and the extra subtotal might clutter. Rejected: the merged band would mix items with different characteristics that are analysed separately, and almost all users supported the subtotal as a comparable anchor. IFRS 18 · para BC150(a)–(c) IFRS 18 · para BC151 Label the middle subtotal EBIT Rejected: EBIT would rarely be faithful — it might imply all interest (including interest revenue) is excluded, or that only interest and tax are excluded. IFRS 18 · para BC149 Present one subtotal when the two are equal Rejected: both subtotals are presented, because identifying each is useful even when equal, particularly for digital access. IFRS 18 · para BC152 Relabel the categories or require a subtotal per category Not required: no category labels and no per-category subtotals (e.g. investing alone), so a different label such as ‘non-operating’ would not change presentation. IFRS 18 · para BC87"
