    "text": "5.1.3 — How the Standard’s examples present operating expenses Example Entity Method Operating lines on the face XYZ ( 4.1.1 , 5.1.1 ) No specified main business activity Mixed Cost of sales, selling, research and development, general and administrative; goodwill impairment by nature; other operating expenses. IFRS 18 · para IE7(b) AA ( 3.8.1 , 5.1.4 ) Manufacturer All by nature Changes in inventories of finished goods and work in progress; raw materials used; employee benefits; depreciation, amortisation and impairment; other operating expenses. IFRS 18 · para IE10 BB ( 3.8.2 ) Manufacturer that provides financing to customers as a main business activity Mixed (function and nature) Cost of sales; interest revenue and interest expenses related to providing financing to customers; selling; research and development; general and administrative; other operating expenses. The example does not label each line’s basis. IFRS 18 · para IE11 CC ( 3.8.3 ) Insurer that invests as a main business activity Mixed (nature and function) Insurance service expenses; credit impairment losses; insurance finance expenses; other operating expenses. EY reads insurance service expenses as a function line required by IFRS 17 ( 5.3 ). IFRS 18 · para IE12 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.4.2.B · Q3-43 DD ( 3.8.4 ) Investment and retail bank All by nature Interest expenses; fee and commission expenses; credit impairment losses; employee benefits; depreciation and amortisation; other operating expenses. IFRS 18 · para IE13 Illustration: 5.1.4 — AA Group — all operating expenses by nature IFRS 18 · para IE10 IFRS 18 · para B84 Facts AA Group (IE10) is a manufacturer that does not invest in assets or provide financing to customers as a main business activity. Applying paragraphs 78, B80–B82 and B85, AA concludes that presenting all operating expenses by nature is the most useful structured summary, because its main drivers of profitability are costs for raw materials and employment. Assessment B80(a) drives the choice: the profitability drivers are themselves natures. Nature amounts presented need not be the period’s expense; the line for changes in inventories of finished goods and work in progress reconciles them to expenses recognised (B84(a), IAS 2.39). No line is classified by function, so paragraphs 82 and 83 do not apply. Decision Present five nature lines, including the changes-in-inventories line; no cost-of-sales line and no paragraph 83 note. AA operating band (in thousands of CU) 20X2 20X1 Revenue 398,700 370,900 Changes in inventories of finished goods and work in progress 3,000 (3,700) Raw materials used (146,000) (143,200) Employee benefits (107,000) (104,600) Depreciation, amortisation and impairment (37,500) (36,300) Other operating expenses (17,100) (15,200) Operating profit 94,100 67,900"
