    "text": "5.1.5 — Why mixed is allowed, and its limits Point Detail Users’ concern Mixed presentations can make it difficult to compare entities, particularly if nature labels do not make clear whether expenses of that nature also sit in function lines. IFRS 18 · para BC250 Why a prohibition was rejected It would block useful information: (i) two types of main business activities; (ii) a manufacturer could not show a substantive PPE impairment separately under function; (iii) goodwill impairment may be arbitrary to allocate, forcing the whole band to nature. It would also clash with the retained IAS 1 list of line items, some of which are natures. IFRS 18 · para BC251(a)–(b) The Board’s answer Mixed is not prohibited; application guidance (B80–B82) protects comparability and faithful representation. IFRS 18 · para BC252 Not a free choice EY view: the most-useful-summary requirement constrains mixed; it would be inappropriate in a presentation by function to present material nature lines in a different location just to present a particular result. IFRS 18 · para 78 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.4.2.E EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.4.2.E Feedback that supported mixed EY notes it avoids arbitrary allocations (e.g. goodwill impairment) and allows separate presentation of expenses whose characteristics aid understanding, such as an impairment expense. EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.4.2.E EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.4.2.E"
