    "chapter": "Chapter 5 — Operating expenses",
    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch05.html#s-5-2",
    "text": "5.2 Function path — cost of sales + qualitative description If any operating line presents expenses classified by function, apply the paragraph 82 face and qualitative package. IFRS 18 · para 82 Function presenters show a separate cost-of-sales line when applicable, although “cost of sales” has no definition. The floor is not costly, scope stays judgemental, each function line needs a qualitative description of natures, the path links to gross profit, and the Big4 align on it. Separate cost-of-sales line (when applicable). Present a separate line item for cost of sales if the entity classifies operating expenses in functions that include a cost-of-sales function. That line item shall include the total of inventory expense described in IAS 2.38. The requirement builds on IAS 1; cost of sales is kept apart from other function lines because it facilitates metrics such as gross margins. IFRS 18 · para 82(a) IFRS 18 · para BC255 IFRS 18 · para BC257 No definition of “cost of sales”. The Board acknowledged diversity in what entities put in cost of sales but decided not to define the term: it could not be done within a reasonable time frame, the topic ranked low in the Third Agenda Consultation, and a definition could have unintended IAS 2 consequences. The IAS 2.38 inventory-expense inclusion is the comparability floor — some entities had previously excluded components of inventory expense from cost of sales. IFRS 18 · para BC256–BC257 KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.2 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.4.2.C EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.4.2.C Why the floor is not costly. IAS 2 already requires an entity to recognise inventories as an expense when the related revenue is recognised and to disclose the amount so recognised. IFRS 18 · para BC257(a)–(b) Scope stays judgemental. EY view: without a definition, entities still consider facts and circumstances for items such as shipping and handling costs and write-offs of inventory and PPE. EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.4.2.C EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.4.2.C Qualitative description of natures in each function line. Disclose a qualitative description of the nature of expenses included in each function line item — to enhance understandability of composition and help users assess comparability of cost of sales across entities. Nature lines need no such description because their composition is typically clear, except “other” expenses ( Ch2 2.4 ). IFRS 18 · para 82(b) IFRS 18 · para BC258 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.4.2.B EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.4.2.B Gross profit link. A cost-of-sales line supports the gross profit subtotal, which paragraph 118 lists as not an MPM ( Ch4 4.4.2 ). IFRS 18 · para 118(a) Big4 alignment. KPMG and EY both stress: any function (or mixed-with-function) face presentation requires separate cost of sales when that function exists, plus qualitative natures per function line; cost of sales is undefined beyond the IAS 2.38 inventory-expense inclusion. KPMG FI 2024 · section 2.2 / section 2.2.2 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.4.2.B / section 3.4.2.C Pitfall: Treating cost of sales as an optional label when the entity’s functions include a cost-of-sales function. Paragraph 82(a) requires a separate line in that case, and that line must include IAS 2.38 inventory expense. IFRS 18 · para 82(a) IFRS 18 · para BC255 IFRS 18 · para BC257"
