    "chapter": "Chapter 5 — Operating expenses",
    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch05.html#s-5-2-1",
    "text": "5.2.1 — Non-recurring items in a by-function statement EY works through five questions for entities that, under IAS 1, presented “non-recurring” or “unusual” items separately inside a by-function statement. The general disaggregation rules are in Ch2 2.3.3 ; MPMs are in Ch6 . EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.5 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.5 Question Answer 1. Does IFRS 18 prohibit separate presentation? No — and it may require it. Non-recurring income and expenses may have sufficiently dissimilar characteristics; persistence is a characteristic that can warrant separate face presentation; the Basis gives restructuring costs as an example. IFRS 18 · para 41(c) IFRS 18 · para B78(c) IFRS 18 · para B79(h) IFRS 18 · para BC413 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.5 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.5 2. What is “non-recurring”? Inherently judgemental and entity-specific; the Board decided not to define “unusual”. EY view: consider the regulatory perspective in each jurisdiction. IFRS 18 · para BC407–BC412 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.5 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.5 3. Persistence split in a by-function statement? The separated item may become essentially a nature item; mixed is permitted when necessary for a useful structured summary, with faithful labels. EY view: the label “non-recurring” is unlikely to be appropriate because it is non-specific. IFRS 18 · para 78 IFRS 18 · para BC252 IFRS 18 · para B82 IFRS 18 · para 43 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.5 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.5 4. Disaggregate a required function line? May be necessary (paragraph 24); EY view: highly judgemental, and because the split creates an adjusted by-function measure, also consider presenting the unadjusted measure ( Illustration 5.2.2 ). IFRS 18 · para 24 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.5 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.5 5. Additional subtotal excluding non-recurring items? Not prohibited; it must meet paragraph 24 and could be an MPM ( Ch4 4.4.1 ). EY view: the unadjusted subtotal may also be needed, and local law may prohibit such subtotals. IFRS 18 · para 24 IFRS 18 · para BC374 EY Closer look 2026 · section 3.5 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 3.5 Illustration: 5.2.2 — Non-recurring items vs cost of sales EY Closer look 2026 · Ill 3-13 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · Ill 3-9 IFRS 18 · para 78–79 IFRS 18 · para B82 IFRS 18 · para 24 IFRS 18 · para BC374 Facts Entity U experiences a meteor strike on one warehouse, leading to an inventory write-down. Management assesses the event as non-recurring (expenses lack persistence) and has previously used a by-function presentation. U considers it necessary to present the meteor-strike effect separately to provide a useful structured summary. Extract shape (EY Ill 3-13): Revenue → Cost of sales excluding meteor effects → Inventory losses relating to meteor strike → Cost of sales (total) → Gross profit. Assessment Function presentation does not force every operating expense into an unlabelled cost-of-sales line — dissimilar characteristics (persistence) can require separate face lines. EY view — highly judgemental, if a by-nature split would create an adjusted by-function measure, also consider presenting the unadjusted measure, and check local regulatory constraints: Highly judgemental If a by-nature split would create an adjusted by-function measure, also consider presenting the unadjusted measure Check local regulatory constraints An additional subtotal excluding non-recurring items may be presented only if necessary under paragraph 24 — and may also meet the MPM definition (paragraph 117) → Ch6. Decision Keep the function total — present a cost-of-sales line that includes the non-recurring item. Also disaggregate on the face so users see both the function total and the persistence split (still inside operating)."
