    "chapter": "Chapter 6 — Management-defined performance measures (MPMs)",
    "href": "../ifrs-18/draft-v1-linked/ch06.html#s-6-5",
    "text": "6.5 Changes to MPMs If calculation changes, a new MPM is added, a previous MPM ceases, or tax-effect methodology changes: Explain the change and its effects Give reasons Restate comparatives unless impracticable. Selecting an MPM is not an accounting-policy choice — but IAS 8 impracticability ideas still apply to the restatement assessment IFRS 18 · para 124–125 Changes to MPMs have triggers (paragraph 124) and require disclosure. Selecting an MPM is not an accounting-policy choice, although IAS 8 impracticability is still used, and specific rules apply if comparatives are omitted as impracticable. Entities restate so trend information remains usable, a zero adjustment is not a change, timing links back to identification, and ceasing an MPM and ceasing one in the year of adoption each need care. Triggers (paragraph 124). Apply when the entity: Changes how it calculates an MPM; Adds a new MPM; Ceases using a previously disclosed MPM; or Changes how it determines the income-tax effects of reconciling items under paragraph 123(d). IFRS 18 · para 124 Disclose. An explanation that enables users to understand the change, addition or cessation and its effects; The reasons for the change, addition or cessation; and Restated comparative information to reflect the change, addition or cessation unless it is impracticable to do so. IFRS 18 · para 124(a)–(c) Not an accounting-policy choice; IAS 8 impracticability still used. An entity’s selection of a management-defined performance measure is not an accounting policy choice. Nonetheless, in assessing whether restating the comparative information is impracticable, an entity shall apply the requirements in paragraphs 50–53 of HKAS 8. Unlike a policy, an MPM is defined to communicate performance outside the FS, not selected within the confines of IFRS; the disclosures borrow from IAS 8 but IAS 8 itself does not apply. IFRS 18 · para 124(c) IFRS 18 · para BC368 IFRS 18 · para BC388 If comparatives omitted as impracticable. Disclose that fact. IFRS 18 · para 125 Why restate. Trend information matters to users, and management is generally expected to monitor what it needs for comparatives. IFRS 18 · para BC390 A zero adjustment is not a change. If an adjusted measure excludes restructuring and there is no restructuring this period, the nil adjustment does not change the calculation. IFRS 18 · para BC389 Link to identification timing. A prior-period MPM dropped because evidence shows it will not appear in current-period public communications is a change or cessation under paragraph 124; so is a measure dropped because it no longer communicates management’s view or is no longer permitted. IFRS 18 · para B122 IFRS 18 · para BC338 EY Closer look 2026 · section 4.2.2 / section 4.3.5 KPMG FI 2024 · section 3.3 Ceasing an MPM. EY view: restating comparatives generally means the ceased MPM and its reconciliation drop out of the comparatives, but the explanation and reasons for ceasing are still disclosed. IFRS 18 · para 124(a)–(c) EY Closer look 2026 · section 4.3.5 · Q4-7 Ceasing in the year of adoption. EY view: no cessation disclosure is needed for an MPM dropped before the first IFRS 18 FS, because it was never a “previously disclosed” MPM ( Ch8 8.2 ). IFRS 18 · para 124(c) EY Closer look 2026 · section 4.3.5 · Q4-8 Pitfall: Treating MPM selection as an IAS 8 accounting-policy change. It is not — but when restating comparatives for an MPM change, still use IAS 8.50–53 to assess impracticability, and disclose if comparatives are omitted. IFRS 18 · para 124(c) IFRS 18 · para 125"
