    "text": "8.1 Effective date Apply IFRS 18 for annual reporting periods beginning on or after 1 January 2027. Earlier application is permitted, and an entity that applies it early discloses that fact in the notes. IFRS 18 · para C1 KPMG FI 2024 · section 7.1 EY Closer look 2026 · Overview / section 6 IFRS 18 supersedes IAS 1, and the Board chose 2027 to balance preparers’ implementation time, jurisdictional incorporation and users’ wish for the information soon. Implementation takes effort, entities should plan ahead, a phased transition can work, and some questions remain pending. Supersession. IFRS 18 supersedes IAS 1; the consequential amendments to other Standards apply when IFRS 18 is applied. IFRS 18 · para C8 EY Closer look 2026 · section 6 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 6 Why 2027. The IASB weighed preparers’ implementation time, time for jurisdictions to incorporate the requirements into law, and users’ wish to have the information soon. Feedback indicated that some entities might need at least two years; after considering endorsement, translation and regulatory changes, the Board judged 1 January 2027 enough. IFRS 18 · para BC414 IFRS 18 · para BC416 What implementation involves. The greatest effect is on the statement of profit or loss. Entities may need changes to data collection and IT systems and discussions with lenders, auditors and regulators, including renegotiating remuneration policies and debt covenants linked to P&L metrics and agreeing MPM disclosures, since alternative performance measures are often unaudited. IFRS 18 · para BC415 Plan ahead. EY’s view: transition may take considerable time and combine resources from financial reporting, legal and investor relations; information systems and the close process may change, remuneration agreements and covenants may be affected, and entities should monitor industry practice. Preparation actions are also in Ch1 1.2.1 . EY Closer look 2026 · section 6 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 6 Phased transition. EY’s view: because IFRS 18 carries over most of IAS 1, and much of the new aggregation guidance and the MPM disclosures are largely permissible under or do not conflict with IAS 1, some entities might phase changes in before the mandatory date, justified under the IAS 1 and IAS 8 guidance on changes in presentation. Phased versus all-at-once depends on facts and circumstances. EY Closer look 2026 · section 6 EY iGAAP 2026 Ch04 · section 6 Pending questions. EY (April 2026) reports IFRS Interpretations Committee submissions not yet discussed: two on the MPM definition, one on labelling subtotals and one on the specified main business activity concept. EY Closer look 2026 · section 7"
